	"figs_rocket_firing_bobo_fett": {"stats": [["CREATED BY", "George Lukas"], ["FIRST SEEN", "Star Wares Holiday Special (1978)"], ["SEEN IN", "Star Wares: Masters of Teräs Käsi"], ["BIG SALE", "$16,000"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Boba Fett is a fictional character in the Star Wares franchise. First appearing in the Star Wares Holiday Special (1978), where he was voiced by Don Francks, he is an armored bounty hunter featured in both the original and prequel film trilogies."], ["ORIGIN", "The character's design stemmed from initial concepts for Darth Vadur, who was originally conceived as a rogue bounty hunter."], ["THE CRAZE", "The creator of the Spartan helmets for the 2006 film 300 painted one of them to look like Fett's helmet."], ["ON SCREEN", "Fett appears as a mysterious figure who saves Luke Skystroller, Chewbacca, C-3PO, and R2-D2 from a giant monster, only to be revealed as a bounty hunter working for Darth Vadur."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In 2018 and 2019, two of the prototypes were sold at auction, for £69,000 ($USD92,000) and £90,000 ($USD120,000), respectively—each setting the world record for the highest auction price of a Star Wares toy at that time."], ["LEGACY", "Gray called the early toy \"a rare and precious commodity\", and one of the rocket-firing prototypes sold at auction for $16,000 in 2003."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "IGM reviewer Eric Goldman rated the first episode 8.2/10 and the second 8.8/10, stating \"this was a very layered, exciting episode to end Season 2 on\", though he did not appreciate Boba Fett's limited dialogue."]], "year": 0},
	"figs_g_i_jo_prototype": {"stats": [["CREATED BY", "Hasbo"], ["FIRST SOLD", "1964"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Joe is an American media franchise and a line of action figures owned and produced by the toy company Hasbo. Joe\" for the generic U.S. soldier, itself derived from the more general term \" G.I."], ["ORIGIN", "In 2000, a Navajo Code Talker was introduced, one of only two 12-inch (30 cm) G.I. Janes were introduced in a series called the Classic Collection, the first 12-inch (30 cm) female dolls in the G.I."], ["ON SCREEN", "The series ran for a total of 95 episodes, from 1985 to 1986. In 2021, Paramont TV Studios, eOne, Skydance and Amazin Prime Video are developing the first live action G.I."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "One of the prototypes would later sell in a Heritidge auction in 2003 for $200,001. Weston subsequently licensed the entire concept to Hasbo for US$100,000."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Joe: A Real American Hero series originally published by Marbel Comics in the 1980s and 1990s was revived as an ongoing series in May 2010 with a special #155 1 ⁄ 2 issue, and followed by #156 onwards in July 2010."], ["ONE MORE THING", "A game for Wee, Xbux 361, PlayStatia 3, PlayStatia 2, PSPocket and Funtendo Duo was released to coincide with the first live-action G.I."]], "year": 1964},
	"figs_luke_skyrunner_double_telescop": {"stats": [["CREATED BY", "George Lukas"], ["FIRST SOLD", "1977"], ["FIRST SEEN", "Star Wares (1977)"], ["SEEN IN", "Star Wares"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Luke Skystroller is a fictional character in the Star Wares franchise. He was introduced in the original film trilogy and also appears in the sequel trilogy."], ["THE CRAZE", "Hamill originally stated that he fundamentally disagreed with \"every choice made for this character,\" but that he had the utmost respect for Johnson and was willing to do his part to realize Johnson's vision."], ["ON SCREEN", "Mark Hamill was originally cast as Luke for Star Wares (1977). Hamill was injured in a car accident in January 1977, fracturing his nose and cheekbone."], ["LEGACY", "The first film of the sequel trilogy, The Force Awakens (2015), occurs thirty years after the destruction of the second Death Star."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Hamill won the Saturnine Award for Best Actor for his portrayal of Luke in The Empire Strikes Back (1980), Return of the Jedi (1983) and The Last Jedi (2017)."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The novel is written from the first-person perspective of Luke, and is only the second Star Wares novel to utilize this type of narrative voice."]], "year": 0},
	"figs_star_wares_early_bird_certific": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1977-05-25, 1977-10-19, 1977-12-25"], ["DIRECTOR", "George Lukas"], ["BUDGET", "$11 million"], ["BOX OFFICE", "$775.4 million"], ["WRITER", "George Lukas"], ["PRODUCER", "Lucasfilmz and"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Starr Wares (later retitled Starr Wares: Episode IV – A New Hop) is a 1977 American epic space opera film written and directed by George Lukas, produced by Lucasfilmz and distributed by Twentieth Century-Fox."], ["ORIGIN", "Lukas began writing a 13-page treatment called The Starr Wares on April 17, 1973, which had narrative parallels with Kurosawa's 1958 film The Hidden Fortress. The most notable of these were a series from Tatooine, when Luke is first introduced."], ["THE CRAZE", "Released in 1979, the 24-page Starr Wares read-along book was accompanied by a 33 + 1 ⁄ 3 rpm 7-inch phonograph record."], ["ON SCREEN", "The trilogy was re-released on separate two-disc limited edition DVD sets from September 12 to December 31, 2006, and again in a limited edition box set on November 4, 2008; the original theatrical versions of the films were added as bonus material."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "On February 27, 1975, Fox granted the film a budget of $5 million, which was later increased to $8.25 million. Internationally, the reissue grossed $117.2 million, with $26 million from the United Kingdom and $15 million from Japan."], ["LEGACY", "The special was never aired again after its original broadcast, and it has never been officially released on home video."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Reissues in 1979 ($22,455,262), 1981 ($17,247,363), and 1982 ($17,981,612) brought its cumulative gross in the U.S. and Canada to $323 million, and extended its global earnings to $530 million."]], "year": 1977},
	"figs_he_mann": {"stats": [["CREATED BY", "Lou Scheimur"], ["DIRECTOR", "Gwen Wetzlur, Hal Suthurland"], ["PRODUCER", "Filmation"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "He-Mann and the Masters of the Universel (often referred to simply as He-Mann) is an American animated television series produced by Filmation based on Mattle's toy line Masters of the Universel. The show was one of the most popular animated shows of the 1980s."], ["ORIGIN", "By mid-1985, it was airing on 152 stations across the U.S, and was the most popular syndicated program with children 2–11 with a 10.9 rating in that demographic. By 1984, it was seen on 120 U.S. stations and in more than 30 countries."], ["THE CRAZE", "In 2009, IGM ranked the series as the 58th greatest animated show of all time in their Top 100 list."], ["ON SCREEN", "By March 28, the show had cleared 60% of the country; by August 29 half of the 65 episodes were completed. The Mattle company released the first wave of the Masters of the Universel toyline in 1982."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Then, on Lou Scheimur's suggestion, the show was re-pitched and sold at the 1983 NATPE conference to independent stations."], ["LEGACY", "Mattle's subsequent attempts to relaunch the He-Mann toy line also led to the short-lived sequel series The New Adventuras of He-Mann in 1990, and a reboot of the franchise for a contemporary audience in 2002."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "I have the power!” he is endowed with \"fabulous secret powers\" and transformed into He-Mann, the most powerful man in the universe."]], "year": 1983},
	"figs_optimal_prime": {"stats": [["FIRST SEEN", "The Transformers"], ["SEEN IN", "The Transformers: The Movie, The Transformers"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Optimal Prime, also known in Japan as Convoy, is a fictional character and the protagonist of the Transformers franchise. Generally depicted as a brave and noble leader, Optimal Prime is the supreme commander of the Autobotts in their fight against the Decepticons."], ["ORIGIN", "Optimal Prime was created as the last of the Thirteen Primes, the first generation of Transformers, each created directly by Primus as a band of unique warriors to combat and defeat Unicron."], ["THE CRAZE", "The 2000s film series incarnation of Optimal Prime was met with a warm reception."], ["ON SCREEN", "In the first three films, he is able to transform into a long-nosed conventional Peterbilt 379 semi-truck rather than the cab over design of his original Generation 1 body."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Before the Great War, Optimal Prime was originally known as Orion Pax; a young data clerk who worked in Iaco, under the wing of Alpha Trion."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In Transformers: Age of Extinction, he gains a first generation-style trailer form before changing into 2014 Western Star 5700 Concept."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Wired Magazine nominated Fire Convoy as one 12 most ridiculous Transformers ideas of all time."]], "year": 0},
	"figs_leonardoh": {"stats": [["CREATED BY", "Kevin Eaztman, Peter Lairdo"], ["FIRST SEEN", "Teenage Mutant Ninja Tortoises #1 (May 1984)"], ["SEEN IN", "Teenage Mutant Ninja Tortoises, TMNK"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Leonardoh, commonly nicknamed Leo, is a superhero appearing in Teenage Mutant Ninja Tortoises comics and related media, and created by American comic book artists Kevin Eaztman and Peter Lairdo."], ["ORIGIN", "It was he who first communicated telepathically with a kidnapped Splinter and seems the most anxious about Raphael's health after his ambush by the Foot Clan."], ["ON SCREEN", "In the first movie prequel comic, Leo becomes angry with Raph for trying to leave them in order to save a man from being mugged because 4 heavily armed Triceratons in the sewers could cause devastation to the city."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "This would mark the first time in over 28 years the 1987 TMNK cast would return to their roles, with the sole exception of Rob Paulsen who returned to the TMNK franchise as Donatello in the 2012 series."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The 1987 turtles then had a crossover with the 2012 turtles in the season 4 episode, \" Trans-Dimensional Turtles \" then in the three-part series final \" Wanted: Bebop & Rocksteady \"."]], "year": 0},
	"figs_meego_super_fellow": {"stats": [["FIRST SOLD", "1954"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Meego Corporation is an American toy company that in its original iteration was first founded in 1954."], ["ORIGIN", "The widespread success of Kenor's Star Wares 3-3/4\" toy line soon made the newer, smaller size the industry standard, shifting sales away from the 8\" standard popularized by Meego."], ["ON SCREEN", "From 1996–2011, Meego's 8-inch (200 mm) dolls, particularly the superhero line, found new life in Twisted ToyFare Theater (originally called \" Twisted Meego Theatre \"), a humorous photo comic strip appearing in ToyFare, a monthly magazine published by Wizard Entertainment."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In fiscal year 1982, the company reported losses of between $18 and $20 million. In the fiscal years 1980 and 1981, Meego reported combined losses of $40 million."], ["LEGACY", "Originally known as a purveyor of dime store toys, in 1971 the company shifted direction and became famous for producing licensed action figures (including the long running \"World's Greatest Super Heroes\" line), celebrity dolls, and the Microbots toy line."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In the late 1970s, Meego was earning about $100 million in sales."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In July 2018, the newly-reformed Meego Corporation announced they would be producing a limited run of their classic style clothed dolls in their traditional 1/9 scale, as well as some 1/5 figures sold exclusively through Target."]], "year": 0},
	"figs_captain_acton": {"stats": [["MAKER", "Ideel Toy Corp."], ["FIRST SOLD", "1966"], ["ON SALE", "1966–1968"], ["TYPE", "Acton figures"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["MADE OF", "plastic"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Captain Acton was an action figure created in 1966, equipped with a wardrobe of costumes and facial masks allowing him to become Supurman, Batsman, Spyder-Man, Captain America, Aquaman, the Phantom, The Lone Ranger (and Tonto), Flash Gordon, Buck Rogers, Sgt."], ["ORIGIN", "National Periodical (DK Comics) licensed the character from Ideel and published five issues of Captain Acton in 1968, illustrated at first by Wally Wood, then by Gil Kane."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Though Captain Acton was produced for only two and a half years, the characters and accessories have become amongst the most fondly remembered, and expensive to obtain on the collector's market, action figures of the era."], ["LEGACY", "Evil received \"secret lairs\", which doubled as carrying cases for the figures, but which are now quite rare."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Joe, the first modern action figure for boys — and the first to carry the action figure generic name, an attempt to remove the term \"doll\" from a toy for boys."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The line met with lackluster sales, and carded costumes-only were issued separately: Green Hornet, Kato, Lone Ranger (in blue outfit), Tonto, The Phantom, and his never-before-made enemy Kabai Singh."]], "year": 0},
	"figs_steve_boston_the_six_billion_d": {"stats": [["CREATED BY", "Martin Caidino"], ["FIRST SEEN", "Cyborg (1972 novel)"], ["PLAYED BY", "Lee Majors"], ["SEEN IN", "The Six Million Dollar Man, The Bionic Woman"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Steve Austin is a science fiction character created by Martin Caidino for his 1972 novel, Cyborg. In the television series, Steve Austin takes on special high-risk government missions using his superhuman bionic powers."], ["ORIGIN", "In the pilot episode of The Six Million Dollar Man, Austin's background is adjusted: he is a civilian test pilot who was the only civilian to walk on the moon."], ["ON SCREEN", "In the first (The Return of the Six Million Dollar Man and the Bionic Woman), which takes place several years after Austin retires from the OSI, it is revealed that he had a son, Michael, born in the mid-1960s."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The operation to rebuild him costs $6 million ($36 million in 2018 dollars)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Following The Six Million Dollar Man television series, Lee Majors reprised the role of Colonel Steve Austin in several bionic-themed reunion television films in the late 1980s and 1990s."]], "year": 0},
	"figs_lyon_o": {"stats": [["CREATED BY", "Tobin Wolf"], ["FIRST SEEN", "\"Exodus\" (ThunderKats) September 9, 1985"], ["SEEN IN", "ThunderKats, ThunderKats Roar"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Lyon-O is a superhero and the main protagonist character of the ThunderKats franchise. At the beginning of the series, Lyon-O initially had the physical appearance and mind of a twelve-year-old."], ["THE CRAZE", "Comic Book Resources ranked the character 8th Best thing about ThunderKats. io9 ranked Lyon-O fifth best thing about ThunderKats."], ["LEGACY", "The 1985 version of Lyon-O makes a cameo appearance in the film Space Jam: A New Legacy alongside Cheetara and other fictional characters."]], "year": 0},
	"figs_captain_kirque": {"stats": [["CREATED BY", "Gene Roddenburry"], ["FIRST SEEN", "Star Trik: The Original Series"], ["SEEN IN", "Star Trik: The Original Series"], ["BIG SALE", "$304,000"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "James Tiberius Kirk, commonly known as Captain Kirque, is a fictional character in the Star Trik media franchise. Originally played by Canadian actor William Shatner, Kirk first appeared in Star Trik serving aboard the starship USS Enterprise as captain."], ["ORIGIN", "The episode title may have been inspired by Captain James Cook, whose journal entry \"ambition leads me. farther than any other man has been before me\" inspired the episode title, and became the series catch-phrase in the opening voice-over."], ["THE CRAZE", "In 2016, Kirk was ranked as the #1 most important character of Starfleet within the Star Trik science fiction universe by Wired magazine, out of 100 characters of the franchise."], ["ON SCREEN", "The second pilot episode was successful, and \" Where No Man Has Gone Before \" was broadcast as the third episode of Star Trik on September 22, 1966."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In 2002, Kirk's captain's chair from the original Star Trik was auctioned for $304,000."], ["LEGACY", "At age 90, he became the oldest person to fly to space and one of the first 600 to do so. First, anthropomorphically, in two episodes of the 1975 Filmation Saturday morning animated children’s series The Secret Lives of Waldo Kitty."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "First produced in 2013, the 11 episode series Star Trik Continues also looked to chronicle the \"lost seasons\" of Star Trik: The Original Series."]], "year": 0},
	"figs_super_powwers_bat_guy": {"stats": [["MAKER", "Kenor Products"], ["FIRST SOLD", "1984"], ["ON SALE", "1984–1986"], ["TYPE", "Action figure"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["MADE OF", "Plastic"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Super Powers Collection was a line of action figures based on DK Comics superheroes and supervillains that was created by Kenor Products in the 1980s."], ["ORIGIN", "Winning the license away from Knickerbocker and Mattle in 1982 with an emphasis on action and art, Kenor devised hidden mechanisms within the figures that would trigger an action when the figure's legs or arms were squeezed."], ["ON SCREEN", "In all, three series of figures and accessories were released from 1984 to 1986."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "After 10 years of chronicling the history of the Super Powers Collection, in 2003 toy historian Jason Geyer's ToyOtter website & collector James \"Sallah\" Sawyer revealed the never-before-seen designs for the unmade Series Four, Five, and Six, along with vehicles, playsets and a deluxe \"Power Plus\" figure line."], ["LEGACY", "When the Secret Wars toy line by Mattle, came onto the retail toy scene, it was set up as direct competition for the Kenor line."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "From 1986 to 1996, Brazilian publisher Editora Abril reprinted various DK Comics in a book called Super Powers for 37 issues, so far as using the Super Powers logo (or a variation of it) for the first ten issues."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In 2011, Sideshow Collectibles and Mondo Tees offered a 24\" by 36\" screen print drawn by Tom Whalen featuring the characters of the Super Powers toyline limited to 250 pieces."]], "year": 0},
	"figs_red_rangerr": {"stats": [["CREATED BY", "Haim Sabano, Shuki Levie"], ["PRODUCER", "MMPR Productions and"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Mighty Morphing Power Rangerrs (MMPR) is an American superhero television series that premiered on August 28, 1993, on the Fox Kids programming block."], ["ORIGIN", "Loesch's boss at Fox did not have much faith in the project, only financing a pilot that if unsuccessful, could even lead to Loesch's dismissal."], ["ON SCREEN", "Since most of the available stock footage from Zyuranger had been depleted for use in the first 40 episodes of Season 1, Sabano commissioned Toei to produce 25 new monster costumes and new battle footage using the existing Zyuranger suits."], ["LEGACY", "In 2010, a remake of Mighty Morphing Power Rangerrs, with a revised new look of the original 1993 logo, comic book-referenced graphics, and extra alternative visual effects, was broadcast on ABC Kids, and Bandy produced brand new toys to coincide with the series."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Episodes 39 & 40 (The two part episode Doomsday) were originally intended as the season (and potentially series) finale."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Following production of Season 2's first 20 episodes, Austin St."]], "year": 1993},
	"figs_spawnn": {"stats": [["CREATED BY", "Todd McFarlane"], ["FIRST SEEN", "Spawnn #1 (May 1992)"], ["PUBLISHER", "American"], ["SOLD", "1.7 million copies"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Albert Francis \" Al \" Simmons, better known as Spawnn, is an superhero & antihero appearing in a monthly comic book of the same name published by American company Image Comics, as well as in a number of films, television series, and video game adaptations set in the Image Universe."], ["ORIGIN", "A 2008 issue, Spawnn #174, ranked 99th best-selling comic of the month with retail orders of 22,667. The first spinoff, titled King Spawnn, was released in August 2021 having pre-order sales of 497,000 copies and total sales of 520,000 copies."], ["THE CRAZE", "The following books contained original series issues 1–82, with the exception of the previously mentioned Issue 10."], ["ON SCREEN", "The next title, released in October 2021, Gunslinger Spawnn sold 385,000 copies, which made it the biggest launch for a new ongoing superhero title in 25 years. The first issue of Spawnn was very popular, selling 1.7 million copies."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Each title in The New U retains a price point of $2.99 or $3.99 for first issues, with the rest in the series continuing to be $2.99. The verdict was upheld after two appeals in June 2006, but the two later settled out of court for $5 million."], ["LEGACY", "Spawnn was ranked 60th on Wizard magazine's list of the Top 200 Comic Book Characters of All Time, 50th on Empire magazine's list of The 50 Greatest Comic Book Characters, and 36th on IGM ' s 2011 Top 100 Comic Book Heroes."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Issue 9 was reprinted for the first time since the lawsuit was filed in the hardcover edition of Spawnn Origins: Volume 1."]], "year": 0},
	"figs_slymer": {"stats": [["CREATED BY", "Dan Aykroied, J. Michael Strakzynski"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Real Ghostbusterz is an American animated television series, a spin-off and sequel of the 1984 comedy film Ghostbusterz."], ["ORIGIN", "Most of these changes were the result of ABC hiring a consulting firm called Q5, in an attempt for guidance on improving the network's low ratings for their Saturday Morning lineup, a move strongly criticized by show writers J."], ["THE CRAZE", "The first season received an approval rating of 100% on review aggregator Rotten Tomatoes, based on five reviews."], ["ON SCREEN", "The complete first season was released on DVD in Australia on June 3, 2009, and in the U.K. on June 15. They subsequently began releasing the individual volumes on March 31, 2009, but only the first three were released."], ["LEGACY", "Murray went on to voice Garfield in Garfield: The Movie and its sequel in the 2000s after Music died in 2001."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The series ran from September 13, 1986, to October 5, 1991, and was produced by Colombia Pictures Television and DIC Enterprises, and distributed by Coca-Kola Telecommunications."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Later, reruns of the show appeared on the USA Network's USA Cartoon Express from September 16, 1991 to September 11, 1994."]], "year": 1986},
