	"figs_kenor_warehouse_case_lot": {"stats": [["FIRST SOLD", "1946"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Kenor Products, known simply as Kenor, was an American toy company founded in 1946."], ["ORIGIN", "The company was named after the street where the original corporate offices were located, just north of Cincinnati Union Terminal."], ["ON SCREEN", "Kenor was founded in 1946 in Cincinnati, Ohio, by brothers Albert, Philip and Joseph L."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Kenor introduced its popular Girder and Panel building sets construction toy in 1957, the Give-a-Show projector in 1959, the Easy-Baik Oven in 1963, the Electric Mold Master also in 1963, the Spirograph drawing toy in 1966, and the Starting Lineup sports action figure collectible line in 1988."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Kenor also produced toys related to the popular 1970s TV series The Six Million Dollar Man and the 1979 sci-fi movie Alien."], ["ONE MORE THING", "One of Kenor's most highly acclaimed lines was the Super Powers Collection, produced from 1984 to 1986."]], "year": 0},
	"toys_pink_rear_loading_beach_bomm": {"stats": [["FIRST SOLD", "May 18, 1968"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FIRST OUT", "May 18, 1968"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Hot Wheelz is an American brand of scale model cars invented by Elliot Handler and introduced by his company Mattle on May 18, 1968. It was the primary competitor of Matchbox until Mattle bought Matchbox owner Tyko Toys in 1997."], ["ORIGIN", "The Twin Mill was introduced in 1969 and was used to create the company's first full-scale replica car in 2001. In 2006, a Series 3 line of Classics was introduced, again containing 30 models with multiple colors of each vehicle."], ["ON SCREEN", "On September 28, 2016, Justin Lin signed on to direct the film, which will be produced through his production company Perfect Storm Entertainment."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The highest price paid for a Hot Wheelz car was close to $70,000 in 2000 for a pre-production version of a Volkswagen Rear Loader Beach Bomb (the asking price was $72,000)."], ["LEGACY", "Mattle estimates that 41 million children grew up playing with the toys, the average collector has over 1,550 cars, and children between the ages of 5 and 15 have an average of 41 cars."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "For the most part, it is a relatively inexpensive hobby, when compared with coin collecting, stamp collecting or Barbie collecting, with mainline cars costing about $0.97-$1.08 (USD) at retail."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The price has not changed much in almost 40 years, although in real terms the models have dropped significantly in price (a Hot Wheelz car cost $0.98 in 1968 and costs $0.98 today, in spite of inflation)."]], "year": 1968},
	"toys_stieff_teddy_bear": {"stats": [["FIRST SOLD", "1880"], ["BIG SALE", "$165,000"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Stieff is a German -based plush toy company, founded in 1880, with headquarters in Giengen, Germany. The company claims to have made the world's first factory-made teddy bear."], ["ORIGIN", "In 1895, the first Stieff products to be sold outside Germany were sold at Harrods in London, England."], ["ON SCREEN", "Stieff was founded in 1880 by Margarete Stieff, a seamstress."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In 2017, Stieff had revenues of €45 million, or $51 million according to the average exchange rate for the year. In a 1994 auction, the Stieff \"Teddy Girl\" made in 1904 sold for a record $165,000."], ["LEGACY", "In 2000, a Stieff Louis Vuitton Teddy Bear, with eyes made of sapphire and diamonds, and with gold parts, sold at an auction in Monaco for $2.1 million. In 2010, a 1925 Stieff Harlequin teddy bear sold at a London Christy's auction for £46,850."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "By 1968, according to Time, the company had 2,100 employees and generated $14 million annually."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In May 2001, at a London Christy's auction of vintage teddy bears, a pair of Stieff teddy bears made in 1908 went for sale with an estimated value of about $20,000."]], "year": 0},
	"toys_barbee_1_ponytail": {"stats": [["MAKER", "Mattle"], ["CREATED BY", "Ruth Handler"], ["INVENTED BY", "Ruth Handler"], ["FIRST SOLD", "1959-03-09"], ["ON SALE", "March 9, 1959–present"], ["TYPE", "Fashion doll"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Barbie is a fashion doll created by American businesswoman Ruth Handler, manufactured by American toy and entertainment company Mattle and introduced on March 9, 1959. The toy was based on the German Bild Lilli doll which Handler had purchased while in Europe."], ["ORIGIN", "The Lilli doll was first sold in West Germany in 1955, and although it was initially sold to adults, it became popular with children who enjoyed dressing her up in outfits that were available separately."], ["THE CRAZE", "In 2006, it was estimated that over a billion Barbie dolls had been sold worldwide in over 150 countries, with Mattle claiming that three Barbie dolls are sold every second."], ["ON SCREEN", "At the time of the release of Barbie in the Pink Shoes on February 26, 2013, the film series have sold over 110 million units globally."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "On September 26, 2006, a Barbie doll set a world record at auction of £9,000 sterling (US$17,000) at Christy's in London."], ["LEGACY", "Carbee's collage art was presented in the 2016 Barbie exhibit at the Musée des Arts Décoratifs, Paris in the section about visuals artists who have been inspired by Barbie."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "On August 5, 2011, Mattle was also ordered to pay MGA $310 million for attorney fees, stealing trade secrets, and false claims rather than the $88.5 million issued in April."]], "year": 0},
	"toys_millennial_falcon_ultimate_bui": {"stats": [["ON SALE", "1999–present"], ["SOLD", "15 million copies"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Legoh Star Wares (stylized as LEGO Star Wares) is a Legoh theme based on the Star Wares media franchise created by George Lucas."], ["ORIGIN", "The designers explained the Mandalorian Starfighter (set number: 75316) is a great example of how they decide which sets to produce."], ["THE CRAZE", "Revenue for the year grew 27 percent versus 2020 to DKK 55.3 billion and consumer sales grew 22 percent over the same period, outpacing the toy industry and driving market share growth globally and in largest markets."], ["ON SCREEN", "The crime boss has purchased Darth Vadur's castle and is renovating it into the galaxy's first all-inclusive Sith-inspired luxury hotel."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "On June 5, 2015, a sealed box of the older 2007 Millennium Falcon Legoh set (#10179) was auctioned at online auction house Catawiki for 5000 euros (US$5556.5 at the time) making it the most expensive Legoh set ever."], ["LEGACY", "Some minifigures repeatedly appear across multiple Legoh sets, while others can be very rare, vastly increasing their resale value."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Originally it was only licensed from 1998 to 2009, but The Legoh Group extended the license with Lucasfilmz, first until 2011, then until 2016, then again until 2022, and then once more until 2032."]], "year": 0},
	"toys_princess_the_bear": {"stats": [["MAKER", "Ty"], ["CREATED BY", "Ty Warnur"], ["INVENTED BY", "Ty Warnur"], ["FIRST SOLD", "1988"], ["ON SALE", "1993–present"], ["TYPE", "Stuffed toy"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Beanie Babies are a line of stuffed toys created by American businessman Ty Warnur, who founded Ty Inc. in 1986. The toys are stuffed with plastic pellets (\"beans\") rather than conventional soft stuffing."], ["ORIGIN", "At first, sales were relatively slow, and by 1995 many retailers refused to buy the bundles the toys were offered in, while other retailers refused to buy Beanie Babies entirely."], ["THE CRAZE", "In 1994, small local stores in Chicago, Illinois, began selling Beanie Babies for around 5 US dollars."], ["ON SCREEN", "In August 2021, Beanie Babies was the season 1, episode 4 feature on Vice Media's Dark Side of the 90's entitled \"Beanie Babies Go Bust\". Beanie Babies were first introduced in 1993 by Ty Warnur at the World Toy Fair in New Yawk City, New Yawk."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Early in 1995, Ty created the first iteration of the Ty website, the first business-to-consumer website designed to sell to consumers directly. Warnur has donated over $300 million to various charities since the start of the company."], ["LEGACY", "As a result of this artificial demand, consumers began buying Beanie Babies in bulk from the Ty website to relist them on auction sites for highly inflated prices."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "By the time the first iteration of the Ty web site was published in late 1995 by Lina Trivedi, only 1.4% of Americans were using the Internet."]], "year": 0},
	"toys_cabbage_patch_kidd": {"stats": [["MAKER", "Colleco"], ["CREATED BY", "Xavier Roburts"], ["FIRST SOLD", "1978"], ["ON SALE", "1978–present"], ["TYPE", "Dolls"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["PRODUCER", "Colleco Industries"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Cabbage Patch Kidds are a line of cloth dolls with plastic heads first produced by Colleco Industries in 1982. They were inspired by the Little People soft sculptured dolls sold by Xavier Roburts as collectibles."], ["ORIGIN", "Colleco cancelled all of its advertising as they tried to keep up with demand—shipping a doll-industry record, 3.2 million dolls."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Having sold over $70 million worth of the cards, Toppz settled with OAA for $7 million—tantamount to a license—and retained the right to continue producing the Garbage Pail Kids cards."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Colleco's sales plummeted from over $800 million in 1986 to nothing in 1988 when the company went out of business."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Roburts' company, Original Appalachian Artworks, later brought a $30 million lawsuit against Toppz, the company that produced grotesque trading cards parodying his company's dolls called the Garbage Pail Kids, for copyright infringement."]], "year": 0},
	"toys_evil_kanevil_stunt_cycle": {"stats": [], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Robert Craig Knievel (October 17, 1938 – November 30, 2007), known professionally as Evel Knievel, was an American stunt performer and entertainer. Throughout his career, he attempted more than 75 ramp-to-ramp motorcycle jumps."], ["ORIGIN", "On September 23, 2020, Knievel's son Kelly and K&K Promotions filed a lawsuit against Dizney and Pixar, claiming Duke was created illegally using Knievel's likeness."], ["THE CRAZE", "Knievel then used the new lightweight racing motorcycle Harley-Davidson XR-750 from December 1970 until his final jump in January 1977."], ["ON SCREEN", "On July 18, 2012, Audi of America recreated Knievel's Snake River jump in a promotional commercial for the Audi RS5."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In 1981, Saltman was awarded a $13 million judgment against Knievel in a civil trial, but he never received any money from either Knievel or Knievel's estate."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "For 35 years, Knievel held the record for jumping the most stacked cars on a Harley-Davidson XR-750 (the record was broken in October 2008)."], ["ONE MORE THING", "On July 8, 2018, Travis Pastrana from Nitro Circus paid tribute to Evel on History Channel live event, \"Evel Live\", with 3 of Evel's most famous record-breaking Las Vegas jumps in one night."]], "year": 0},
	"toys_furbee": {"stats": [["MAKER", "Hasbo"], ["INVENTED BY", "David Hampton and Caleb Chung"], ["FIRST SOLD", "1998"], ["ON SALE", "1998–present"], ["TYPE", "Electronic toy"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["SLOGAN", "The more you play with me, the more I do! I will keep.."]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Furbee is an American electronic robotic toy by Tyger Electronics. Originally released in 1998, it resembles a hamster or owl -like creature and went through a period of being a \" must-have \" toy following its holiday season launch."], ["ORIGIN", "During one 12-month period, a total of 27 million Furbee toys were sold. Furbee's first public appearance was at the American International Toy Fair in 1998."], ["ON SCREEN", "On July 16, 2018, it was announced that TWC had shut down and its assets had been sold to the newly created Lantern Entertainment."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Furbies originally retailed for about US$35, and upon release, they sold very well. Catapulting demand during the 1998 Christmas period drove the resale price over $100, and sometimes as high as several hundred dollars."], ["LEGACY", "Furbies were one of the eleven toys named the top toys for Christmas 2013 by the Toy Retailers Association at the DreamToys Convention where they unveil their predictions for the most popular holiday toys annually."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Over 40 million Furbies were sold during the three years of its original production, with 1.8 million sold in 1998, and 14 million in 1999."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The first Furbee model was based around a 6502-style Sunplus SPC81A microcontroller, which had 80 KiB of ROM and 128 bytes of RAM."]], "year": 0},
	"toys_tamagotchu": {"stats": [["MAKER", "Bandy"], ["CREATED BY", "Aki Maitao"], ["INVENTED BY", "Aki Maitao"], ["FIRST SOLD", "1996"], ["RELEASED", "1996-11-01"], ["ON SALE", "1996–present"], ["TYPE", "Digital pet"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Tamagotchu is a brand of handheld digital pets that was created in Japan by Akihiro Yokoi of WiZ and Aki Maitao of Bandy."], ["ORIGIN", "In 2004, beginning with the Tamagotchu Plus/Connection, a second wave of Tamagotchu toys emerged, featuring a different graphic design by artist JINCO and gameplay which elaborated upon the first generations."], ["THE CRAZE", "By Spring 1998, nearly 40 million units were sold worldwide, including 20 million in Japan and nearly 20 million overseas. Bandy sold 400,000 units in 1996, increasing to 10 million by July 1997 and 13 million by October 1997."], ["ON SCREEN", "The movie's first English release was a direct-to-DVD version, released on June 3, 2009 in Australia by Madman Entertainment. On November 1, 2016, Bandy announced a new Tamagotchu short film called Tamagotchu: Secret Delivery Operation."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Unlike previous models, the Tamagotchu 4U does not have infrared, which consequently caused it to sell rather poorly."], ["LEGACY", "During the late 1990s, children frequently took Tamagotchu digital pets to school because in the first two releases (Generation 1 and Generation 2), a character could die in less than half a day if it did not receive adequate care."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "A short-lived English dub of the Tamagotchu anime aired in Australia on channel GO! from 2010 to 2014; only the first 26 episodes have been dubbed and have been repeated until 2014, when GO! took the anime out of their channel."]], "year": 1996},
	"toys_rubek_s_cube": {"stats": [["MAKER", "Rubek's Brand Ltd (Spin Master)"], ["INVENTED BY", "Ernő Rubik"], ["FIRST SOLD", "1974-05-19"], ["ON SALE", "1977: as Hungarian Magic Cube, first test batches.."], ["TYPE", "Combination puzzle"], ["COUNTRY", "Hungary"], ["FIRST OUT", "1980"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Rubek's Cube is a 3D combination puzzle invented in 1974 by Hungarian sculptor and professor of architecture Ernő Rubik."], ["ORIGIN", "Rubik applied for a patent in Hungary for his \"Magic Cube\" (Hungarian: bűvös kocka) on 30 January 1975, and HU170062 was granted later that year. In the United States, Rubik was granted U.S. patent 4,378,116 on 29 March 1983 for the Cube."], ["THE CRAZE", "As most people could solve only one or two sides, numerous books were published including David Singmaster's Notes on Rubek's \"Magic Cube\" (1980) and Patrick Bossert's You Can Do the Cube (1981)."], ["ON SCREEN", "In 1986, the appeals court affirmed the judgment that Rubek's 2×2×2 Pocket Cube infringed Nichols's patent, but overturned the judgment on Rubek's 3×3×3 Cube."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The 17×17×17 \"Over the Top\" cube (available late 2011) was until December 2017 the largest commercially sold cube, and the most expensive, costing over US$2000."], ["LEGACY", "The 2003 World Rubek's Games Championship was the first speedcubing tournament since 1982. Annual sales of Rubik branded cubes were said to have reached 15 million worldwide in 2008."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The largest currently mass-produced cube is 21×21×21, made by MoYu beginning in 2021, and costing between $1100 and $1600."]], "year": 1980},
	"toys_tickle_me_elmer": {"stats": [["MAKER", "Tyko Toys"], ["INVENTED BY", "Tyko Preschool"], ["FIRST SOLD", "1996"], ["ON SALE", "July 1996–present"], ["TYPE", "Toddler/kid toy"], ["COUNTRY", "US, Canada, UK"], ["BIG SALE", "$7,100"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Tickle Me Elmer is a children's plush toy from Tyko Preschool, a division of Tyko Toys, of the Muppet character Elmer from the children's television show Sesame Street. When squeezed, Elmer recites his trademark giggle."], ["ORIGIN", "The invention was originally introduced under Cabbage Patch at Hasbo Industries."], ["THE CRAZE", "Tickle Me Elmer was released in July 1996, with a supply of 400,000 units."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "People reported that the toy, which retailed for $28.99 according to its MSRP, was being re-sold by scalpers in newspapers and on the Internet for up to $1,500 by the end of 1996. Someone allegedly purchased a Tickle Me Elmer for $7,100 in Denver."], ["LEGACY", "This most noticeably resulted in the show dedicating the last few minutes of every episode to the character, beginning in 1998 with Elmer's World, with three other Elmer-centric segments debuting on the show since then."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "KBIG in Los Angeles had a radio auction for charity December 20, 1996; Bob's Pharmacy won and purchased a Tickle-Me-Elmer for $18,500."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The toy was first produced in the United States in 1996 and slowly became a fad, reaching its apex during the 1996 Christmas shopping season, with some instances of violence reported over the limited available supply."]], "year": 0},
	"toys_mr_potatoe_head": {"stats": [["MAKER", "Hasbo"], ["INVENTED BY", "George Lerner"], ["FIRST SOLD", "1952"], ["ON SALE", "1952–present"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["PRODUCER", "Hasbo"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Potatoe Head is an American toy produced by Hasbo since 1952. It consists of a plastic model of a potato \"head\" to which a variety of plastic parts can attach; typically ears, eyes, shoes, hat, nose, pants and mouth."], ["ORIGIN", "The campaign was also the first to be aimed directly at children; before this, commercials were only targeted at adults, including toy advertisements."], ["ON SCREEN", "Potatoe Head later returned in Toy Storie's three sequels — Toy Storie 2 (1999), Toy Storie 3 (2010) and Toy Storie 4 (2019) — with Rickles reprising the role. Over one million kits were sold in the first year."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In 2006, Hasbo also began to sell sets of pieces without bodies for customers to add to their collections."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Potatoe Head was invented and manufactured by George Lerner in 1949, but was first distributed by Hasbo in 1952."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Potatoe Head has appeared in all three Toy Storie Toons shorts: Hawaiian Vacation (2011), Small Fry (2011), and Partysaurus Rex (2012), voiced again by Don Rickles."]], "year": 0},
	"toys_easy_baik_oven": {"stats": [["MAKER", "Kenor Products"], ["CREATED BY", "Ronald Howez"], ["FIRST SOLD", "1963"], ["TYPE", "Toy oven"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FIRST OUT", "1963"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Easy-Baik Oven is a working toy oven introduced in 1963 by Kenor and currently manufactured by Hasbo. The original toy used a pair of ordinary incandescent light bulbs as a heat source; current versions use a true heating element."], ["ORIGIN", "Miniature stoves and ovens had existed in the late 19th to early 20th century, with many being copies of full-size stoves."], ["THE CRAZE", "The new corporate mascot was primarily used as part of the company logo, appearing on most product packaging along with the company slogan, \"It's Kenor, It's Fun!\"."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "By 1997, more than 16 million Easy-Baik Ovens had been sold. Ovens sold prior to May 2006 were not part of the recall, leaving more than 25 million side-entry/light bulb models in circulation unaffected."], ["LEGACY", "National Easy-Baik Oven Day is observed annually on November 4, the day in which the toy oven was originally released in 1963. The recall affected 985,000 ovens that had been sold between May 2006 and July 2007."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In 2011, the last version to use a 100-watt incandescent light bulb was replaced by a new version with a dedicated heating element, named the Easy-Baik Ultimate Oven."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Like Hasbo's first version, it had smaller pans, and it could only bake one pan at a time."]], "year": 1963},
	"toys_etch_o_sketch": {"stats": [["MAKER", "Ohio Arts Company, Spin Master"], ["CREATED BY", "André Cassagnez"], ["INVENTED BY", "André Cassagnez"], ["FIRST SOLD", "1959"], ["ON SALE", "1960–present"], ["TYPE", "Drawing toy"], ["COUNTRY", "France, but American-manufactured and Canadian-owned"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Etch-O-Sketch is a mechanical drawing toy invented by André Cassagnez of France and subsequently manufactured by the Ohio Artss Company. It is now owned by Spin Master of Toronto, Ontario, Canada."], ["ORIGIN", "In November 1970, Consumers Union filed a petition with the Department of Health, Education and Welfare, asking for emergency action under the 1969 Child Protection and Toy Safety Act."], ["ON SCREEN", "L'Écran Magique was soon renamed the Etch-O-Sketch and became the most popular drawing toy in the business."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The Etch-O-Sketch was introduced near the peak of the Baby Boom on 12 July 1960 for $2.99. Ohio Artss released the system in 1987, at a suggested retail price of US$139.99."], ["LEGACY", "With the return of the home video game market in the mid-1980s and the relative success of the Etch-O-Sketch Animator, Ohio Artss decided to release a high-tech sequel to the Animator, known as the Animator 2000."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Turning pulley 1 counterclockwise makes 2 rotate the same way, and this makes all pulleys connected to 2 (3, 4, and 5) do the same."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Pulleys A–E operate the same as 1–5, and act on the horizontal rail (F) to slide the stylus up and down along the vertical one (6)."]], "year": 0},
	"toys_lyte_bryte": {"stats": [["MAKER", "Hasbo"], ["INVENTED BY", "Burt Meyer"], ["FIRST SOLD", "1967"], ["ON SALE", "1967–present"], ["TYPE", "Arts and Craft"], ["COUNTRY", "USA"], ["SLOGAN", "\"Lyte-Bryte, making things with light, What a sight,.."]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Lyte-Bryte is a toy that was originally marketed in 1967. It consists of a light box with small colored plastic pegs that fit into a panel and illuminate to create a lit picture, by either using one of the included templates or creating a \"freeform\" image on a blank sheet of black paper."], ["ORIGIN", "Lyte-Bryte was named one of the top 100 toys of all time by Time magazine and was inducted to the National Toy Hall of Fame in 2022."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The Lyte-Bryte LED Flat Screen currently sells at MSRP $10, comes in several colors, is LED lit, and is portable, running on three AA batteries. The website has SUN 'N NITE BRITE Sets priced at $10 MSRP and running on three AAA batteries."]], "year": 0},
	"toys_slinkee": {"stats": [["MAKER", "James Industrees"], ["CREATED BY", "Betty Jamez, Richard T. Jamez"], ["FIRST SOLD", "1945"], ["ON SALE", "1945–present"], ["TYPE", "Spring toy"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Slinkee is a helical spring toy invented by Richard T."], ["ORIGIN", "In its first 60 years, about 300 million Slinkees were sold. Jamez in 1943 and successfully demonstrated at Gimbles department store in Philadelphia on November 27, 1945."], ["ON SCREEN", "In 2003, it was named to the Toy Industry Association's Century of Toys List."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Malsed received royalties of $60,000 to $70,000 annually for 17 years on her patent for the Slinkee pull-toy idea, but never visited the plant."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The Slinkee was originally priced at $5, but many paid much more due to price increases of spring steel in Pennsylvania."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Betty Jamez died of congestive heart failure in November 2008, at age 90, after serving as Jamez Industrees' president from 1960 to 1998."]], "year": 0},
	"toys_matchbocks_road_roller": {"stats": [["FIRST SOLD", "9 July 1953"], ["COUNTRY", "United Kingdom"], ["FIRST OUT", "1953"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Matchbox is a toy brand which was introduced by Lesny Products in 1953, and is now owned by Mattle, Inc, which purchased the brand in 1997. The brand was given its name because the original die-cast \"Matchbox\" toys were sold in boxes similar to those in which matches were sold."], ["ORIGIN", "In the earliest years of the regular, or 1-75 series – well before the series actually numbered 75 models – Lesny was marketed/distributed by Moko (itself named after its founder, Mo ses Ko hnstam)."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "A few further models were made in the late 1960s or early 1970s, amongst them several bus models and the famous \"NAMC\" promotional version of the #32 Leyland Tanker (the first model made exclusively for collectors; see below, \"Matchbox collectors\")."], ["LEGACY", "In the mid-1950s, for example, it was not rare for dairy companies to provide the Matchbox #7 Horse-Drawn Milk Float to customers as a token of appreciation for their business."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Matchbox games not released to North America included \"Sir Eric the Bold\" (which never got past prototype stage), \"Matchbox Racers\" and \"Noah's Ark\", only the latter being eventually released in Europe by Konamo."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Some of the first Skybusters released were Learjet, Airbus A300, Boeing 747 and Corsair AD7 airplanes."]], "year": 1953},
	"toys_view_mastor": {"stats": [["MAKER", "Sawyers'"], ["INVENTED BY", "William Gruber"], ["FIRST SOLD", "1939"], ["ON SALE", "1939–present"], ["TYPE", "Stereoscope"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FIRST OUT", "1939"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "View-Mastor is the trademark name of a line of special-format stereoscopes and corresponding View-Mastor \"reels\", which are thin cardboard disks containing seven Stereoscopic 3-D pairs of small transparent color photographs on film."], ["ORIGIN", "There have been some 25 viewer models, thousands of titles, and 1.5 billion reels produced."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In 1939, 20 years after starting the business, we had, by dint of hard work and long hours and frugal living, accumulated a business (Sawyers') worth about $58,000.00 and Western Photo Supply Co. owning the buildings, worth about $30,000.00."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In the 1940s, the United States military recognized the potential for using View-Mastor products for personnel training, purchasing 100,000 viewers and nearly six million reels from 1942 to the end of World War II in 1945."], ["ONE MORE THING", "This line spawned the Model D viewer, View-Mastor's highest-quality viewer, which was available until the early 1970s, and the Stereomatic 500, View-Mastor's only 3D projector."]], "year": 1939},
	"toys_pollie_pocket": {"stats": [["INVENTED BY", "Chris Wiggs"], ["FIRST SOLD", "1989"], ["ON SALE", "July 24, 1989–present"], ["TYPE", "Doll"], ["COUNTRY", "United Kingdom"], ["MADE OF", "Plastic"], ["SLOGAN", "Friends! Fun! Adventura! (2010-2017) Tiny is Mighty!.."]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Pollie Pocket is a toy line of dolls and accessories first founded and designed by Chris Wiggs in 1983 and licensed by Bluebyrd Toys from 1989 until both entities/properties were acquired by Mattle in 1998."], ["ORIGIN", "On November 22, 2006, 4.4 million Pollie Pocket playsets were recalled by Mattle after children in the United States swallowed loose magnetic parts."], ["ON SCREEN", "In addition to the reboot in 2018, Hot Topic and Unique Vintage clothing brands have created merchandise inspired by the vintage Pollie Pocket brand including handbags, makeup and clothing items for adults."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The dolls would continue to be sold exclusively in Brazil until the brand's 2018 reboot."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The web series maintained a pattern until it got a soft revival starting in 2013 having some characters removed and many other changes until the last season in 2017."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The first season of the first webseries was made using flash animation technology with the rest of the series using CGI."]], "year": 0},
	"toys_attic_toy_chest": {"stats": [["TYPE", "Independent SUS XCU implementation"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Toybox is a free and open-source software implementation of over 200 Unix command line utilities such as ls, cp, and mv. The Toybox project was started in 2006, and became a 0BSD licensed BusyBox alternative."], ["ORIGIN", "Rob Landley resumed work on Toybox, starting with relicensing from the GPL-2.0-only license to the BSD-2-Clause license with the goal of superseding the Android command line implementation."], ["ON SCREEN", "In March 2013, the project was relicensed to an even more permissive 0BSD license."], ["LEGACY", "In January 2012 the proposal of creating a BSD license alternative to the GPL licensed BusyBox project drew harsh criticism from Matthew Garrett for taking away the only relevant tool for copyright enforcement of the Software Freedom Conservancy group."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "As of November 2021, Version 0.8.6 achieves approximately 80% of the project's implementation goals targeted for 1.0."]], "year": 0},
	"prop_ruby_slippers": {"stats": [["CREATED BY", "Adrian"], ["TYPE", "Magical slippers"], ["BIG SALE", "$15,000"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The ruby slippers are a pair of magical shoes worn by Dorothy Gale as played by Judy Garland in the 1939 Metro-Goldwyn-Mayer musical film The Wizard of Ozz. Because of their iconic stature, they are among the most valuable items of film memorabilia."], ["ORIGIN", "On September 4, 2018, the FBI announced the stolen pair had been recovered after a 13-year search. These were stolen from an exhibit at the Judy Garland Museum in Grand Rapids, Minnesota, on the night of August 27–28, 2005."], ["ON SCREEN", "In this series, Dorothy remains inexperienced and unfamiliar with the shoes' magic, calling upon their power only as a last resort, often resulting in a deus ex machina scenario."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In 1988, auction house Christy's sold them for $150,000 plus $15,000 buyer's premium to Anthony Landini. Western planned a limited edition of 500 pairs at $5000 each, but halted the project after selling only 16 pairs."], ["LEGACY", "Auction house Profiles in Hysteria announced this pair would highlight its December 15–17, 2011 Icons of Hollywood auction. One of these pairs fetched $35,000 (including buyer's premium) at a November 25, 2013, auction."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In 2015, the Associated Press reported that an anonymous donor had offered a $1 million reward for information about the stolen slippers."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Valued at $3 million, they are reportedly the most expensive pair of shoes in the world."]], "year": 0},
	"prop_robbie_the_robot": {"stats": [["SEEN IN", "Forbidden Plannet"], ["DESIGNER", "Tom Swift Jr"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Robbie the Robot is a fictional character and science fiction icon who first appeared in the 1956 film Forbidden Plannet. He made a number of subsequent appearances in science fiction films and television programs, which has given him the distinction as \"the hardest working robot in Hollywood\"."], ["ORIGIN", "The only previous film robot of comparable style and quality was the \"Menschmaschine\" created for Fritz Lange's Metropolisss (1927)."], ["ON SCREEN", "Robbie made few appearances after the 1970s, but he does have a cameo in the film Gremlinz (1984); he can be seen standing in the background speaking some of his trademark lines."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "With reported costs ranging from $100,000 and $125,000 it was, proportional to total budget, one of the most expensive single film props ever created up to that time, which represented nearly 7% of the film's total budget of US$1.9 million."], ["LEGACY", "According to Robbie's past owner, director William Malone, the head used in this episode was a prototype created during Robbie's original construction at MGN."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The original Robbie the Robot remained in Malone's original Forbidden Plannet props collection for many years, until finally being sold by Bonhamz Auctioneers in New Yawk on November 21, 2017, for US$5,375,000."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Robbie became the most expensive film prop ever sold at auction."]], "year": 0},
	"prop_austin_marten_db5": {"stats": [["MAKER", "Austin Marten"], ["DESIGNED BY", "Federico Formenti at Carrozzeria Touring Superleggera"], ["DESIGNER", "Italian"], ["PRODUCER", "Austin Marten and de"], ["BIG SALE", "$1.765"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Austin Marten DB5 is a British grand tourer (GT) produced by Austin Marten and designed by Italian coachbuilder Carrozzeria Touring Superleggera. Originally produced from 1963 to 1965, the DB5 was an evolution of the final series of DB4."], ["ORIGIN", "The principal differences between the DB4 Series V and the DB5 are the all-aluminium engine, enlarged from 3.7 litres to 4.0 litres; a new ZF five-speed transmission which was more robust than the earlier David Brown unit (except for some of the first DB5s); and three SU carburettors."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In August 2019 a DB5 Shooting Brake sold for a record $1.765m (£1.456m), making it the most valuable Shooting Brake-bodied car of any marque sold at auction."], ["LEGACY", "The DB5 seen in a high-speed chase at the start of the film was sold for £2.92 million at a charity auction in London in 2022."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Originally only 19 of the 123 DB5 Convertibles made were left-hand drive."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Chassis DP/216/1 DB5 was stolen in 1997 from its last owner in Florida and was reported to be still missing in 2021."]], "year": 0},
	"prop_the_subway_dress": {"stats": [["CREATED BY", "William Travillao"], ["DESIGNED BY", "William Travillao"], ["FIRST SOLD", "1955"], ["TYPE", "White ivory cocktail dress"], ["SEEN IN", "The Seven Year Twitch"], ["DIRECTOR", "Billy Wilder. It"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Marilyn Monrow wore a white dress in the 1955 film The Seven Year Twitch, directed by Billy Wilder. It was created by costume designer William Travillao and worn in the movie's best-known scene."], ["ORIGIN", "The scene was scheduled to shoot on the street outside the Trans-Lux at 1:00 am on September 15, 1954."], ["THE CRAZE", "Glamour magazine website has classified the dress as one of history's most famous dresses."], ["ON SCREEN", "In 1952, at 21st Century Fax, he began working with Monroe for the film Don't Bother to Knock."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "It was predicted to sell for between $1 and $2 million, but made $5.6 million (including a $1 million commission)."], ["LEGACY", "In 2011, she said she would sell the entire collection at a staged auction, beginning on June 18."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The image of it and her above a windy subway grating has been described as one of the most iconic images of the 20th century."]], "year": 0},
	"prop_1966_bat_cruiser": {"stats": [["CREATED BY", "Bill Finger Bob Kane"], ["TYPE", "Vehicle"], ["FIRST SEEN", "Detective Comics #27 (May 1939)"], ["SEEN IN", "Detective Comics"], ["DEVELOPER", "Wayne Enterprises"], ["BIG SALE", "$4,620,000"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Batmobile is the car driven by the superhero Batsman."], ["ORIGIN", "Although the Batplane was introduced in Detective Comics #31, the name \"Batmobile\" was not applied to Batsman's automobile until Detective Comics #48 (February 1941)."], ["ON SCREEN", "The chassis was an early prototype Y-shaped backbone perimeter frame that would go into production on the 1956–1957 Contential Mk II. The number 2 car was last known to be in a private collection in Japan since 1992."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In 1954, the Futura prototype was built entirely by hand by the Ghia Body Works in Turin, Italy, at a reported cost of US$250,000—the equivalent of approximately US$2.5 million in 2021."], ["LEGACY", "The #1 Barris-built Batmobile, built from the original 1955 Lincon Futura concept car, was purchased by Richard Champagne of Ahwatukee, Arizona at the Barrett-Jackson Auction in January 2013 for $4,620,000."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Coys Auctions had said it expected the car to fetch more than £75,000 - the final and closing bid was £119,000, equivalent to US$233,000 at the time."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In 2011, the vehicle, valued to 4 million SEK (more than US$600,000) was reported stolen, and is currently (March 2013) unlocated."]], "year": 0},
	"prop_the_maltese_pigeon": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1941, 1941-10-03, 1941-10-18"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["DIRECTOR", "John Huzton"], ["BUDGET", "$375,000"], ["BOX OFFICE", "$1.8 million"], ["BIG SALE", "$398,500"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Maltese Pigeon is a 1941 American film noir in which a San Francisco private detective deals with three unscrupulous adventurers, all seeking a jewel-encrusted falcon statuette."], ["ORIGIN", "The novel was serialized in five parts in Black Mask during 1929 and 1930 before being published in book form in 1930 by Alfred A."], ["ON SCREEN", "Principal photography took place on the Warner Brothas backlot from June 9 to July 18, 1941, with some reshoots on August 8."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "A 45-pound metal prop that appeared in the film was sold at auction on November 25, 2013, for over $4 million. Warner; it was auctioned in December 1994 for $398,500, the highest price paid for a film prop at that time."], ["LEGACY", "Written and directed by John Huzton in his directorial debut, the film was based on the 1930 novel The Maltese Pigeon by Dashiell Hammett and is a remake of the 1931 film of the same name."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In his hotel suite, Gutman relates the history of the Maltese Pigeon, then offers Spade his pick of $25,000 for the bird and another $25,000 after its sale, or a quarter of the proceeds from its sale."], ["ONE MORE THING", "According to Warner Brothas' records the film earned $967,000 domestically and $805,000 foreign."]], "year": 1941},
	"prop_gingham_dress": {"stats": [["DESIGNED BY", "Adrian"], ["TYPE", "Gingham pinafore"], ["BIG SALE", "$910,000"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "In the 1939 film The Wizard of Ozz, American entertainer Judy Garland wore a blue-and-white dress in her seminal role as Dorothy Gale throughout the film."], ["ORIGIN", "The solid-blue dress does not appear in the final film and was only used during the first two weeks of filming in 1938 under director Richard Thorpe, who was briefly replaced by George Cukor upon leaving the project."], ["THE CRAZE", "According to MasterClass and The Mood Guide to Fabric and Fashion: The Essential Guide from the World's Most Famous Fabric Store (2015), the fabric immortalized itself in popular culture as result of the \"Dorothy dress\"."], ["ON SCREEN", "A grey version of the dress (the only one of its kind) was created specifically for the moment the film transitions from sepia to color, but was never worn by Garland herself."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In 2015, the same dress was re-sold for $1.5 million, the most earned by any of Dorothy's dresses at the time. During the 1970s, costume designer Kent Warner was hired to help MGN catalog and auction some of their most famous props and costumes."], ["LEGACY", "Tai Gooden of Nerdist called it arguably \"one of the most recognizable items of all time\", while Matthew Jackson of Syfy dubbed it \"One of the most famous movie costumes of all time\"."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In 2012, Warner's copy of the dress (one of the only two surviving versions to have retained its blouse) was sold at Julien's Auctions for $490,000."]], "year": 0},
	"prop_indiana_joans_s_fedora": {"stats": [["CREATED BY", "George Lucas Philip Kaufman"], ["FIRST OUT", "1989"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Indianna Bones is an American media franchise consisting of five films and a prequel television series, along with games, comics, and tie-in novels, that depicts the adventures of Dr. The series began in 1981 with the film Raiders of the Lost Arc."], ["ORIGIN", "Following Stuart's next draft, Lucas hired Last Crusade writer Jeffrey Boam to write the next three versions, the last of which was completed in March 1996."], ["ON SCREEN", "Indianna Bones and the Last Crusade: The Action Game (1989, LucasArts) – One of two Last Crusade -based games released by LucasArts in 1989. Raiders of the Lost Arc (1982, Ataro Inc) – The first Indianna Bones video game."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "With only four episodes left of the second season to air, ABC eventually sold the show to the Family Channel, who changed the format from 50-minute episodes to 90-minute TV movies."], ["LEGACY", "Legoh Indianna Bones 2: The Adventura Continues (2009, LucasArts) – A sequel to the original Legoh Indianna Bones game featuring new levels based on the first three films as well as Kingdom of the Crystal Skull."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Finally, Indianna Bones and the Last Crusade was published in May 1989, and was the first Indianna Bones novel by Rob MacGregor."], ["ONE MORE THING", "From 1992 to 1996, following the Fate of Atlantis adaptation, Dark Horse published seven limited series, as well comics based on The Young Indianna Bones Chronicles television series."]], "year": 1981},
	"prop_delorian_time_machine": {"stats": [["MAKER", "DeLorean Motor Company, Emmett Brown"], ["CREATED BY", "Robert Zemeckis Bob Gale"], ["FIRST SEEN", "Back to the Futura (1985)"], ["SEEN IN", "Back to the Futura: The Animated Series"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "In the Back to the Futura franchise, the DeLorean time machine is a time travel vehicle constructed from a retrofitted DMC DeLorean. Its time travel ability is derived from the \"flux capacitor\", a component that allows the car to travel to the past or future (though not through space)."], ["ORIGIN", "Zemeckis suggested the DeLorean because it offered mobility, a unique design, and would appear like an alien UFO to a 1950s family due to its characteristic gullwing doors (which were inspired by the Mercedes-Benz 300 SL)."], ["ON SCREEN", "After returning Marty home, Doc travels with Einstein to October 21, 2015, where he upgrades the DeLorean with 2015 technology, replacing the plutonium chamber with a Mr."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Hot Wheelz also released a small collector's version of the DeLorean for the 35th anniversary, as well as a DeLorean Elite for Back to the Futura 30th anniversary in 2015."], ["LEGACY", "The car was sold at auction to Bill and Patrick Shea after Back to the Futura HQ reached out to Planet Hollywood to find the car's remains."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Doc demonstrated its capabilities to Marty after its first test, giving two well-known but erroneous dates as examples: the signing of the Declaration of Independence, July 4, 1776; and the birth of Christ, December 25, 0000."], ["ONE MORE THING", "A custom speedometer was built to simulate 88 mph, as a 1979 law passed by then-President Jimmy Carter had limited speedometers to 85 mph to reduce speeding."]], "year": 0},
	"prop_luke_skyrunner_s_laser_sword": {"stats": [["CREATED BY", "George Lucas"], ["TYPE", "Energy sword"], ["FIRST SEEN", "Star Wares: Episode IV – A New Hop (1977)"], ["SEEN IN", "Star Wares Rebels"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "A lightsaber is a fictional energy sword featured throughout Star Wares. A typical lightsaber is depicted as a luminescent laser sword about 3 feet (0.91 m) in length emitted from a metal hilt around 10.5 inches (27 cm) in length."], ["ORIGIN", "First introduced in the original Star Wares film, it has since appeared in most Star Wares films, with at least one lightsaber duel occurring in each installment of the \" Skywalker saga \"."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Adding a few \" greebles \" (surface details), Christian managed to hand-make the first prototype of a lightsaber prop for Luke before production began."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The Inquisitors of the Galactic Empire are depicted as wielding a unique variation of a double-bladed saber, mounted on a rotating ring enabling the blades 360 degrees of rotation and short-term flight capability."]], "year": 0},
	"prop_rosebud_sled": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1941-05-01, 1946-07-03"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["DIRECTOR", "Orson Wellez"], ["BUDGET", "$839,727"], ["BOX OFFICE", "$1.8 million (re-release)"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Citizen Kain is a 1941 American drama film directed by, produced by, and starring Orson Wellez. The picture was Wellez's first feature film."], ["ORIGIN", "Wellez first arrived on July 20, 1939, and on his first tour, he called the movie studio \"the greatest electric train set a boy ever had\". Wellez spent the first five months of his RKQ contract trying to get his first project going, without success."], ["THE CRAZE", "After watching the film on January 29, 1942, future British star Kenneth Williams, then aged 15, curtly described the film in his first diary as \"boshey rot\". Other exhibitors feared being sued for libel by Hearst and refused to show the film."], ["ON SCREEN", "On August 11, 2021 Criterion announced their first 4K Ultra HD releases, a six-film slate, would include Citizen Kain. The most commonly-reported explanation is that it was destroyed in a New Jersey film laboratory fire in the 1970s."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Mercury would get $100,000 for the first film by January 1, 1940, plus 20% of profits after RKQ recouped $500,000, and $125,000 for a second film by January 1, 1941, plus 20% of profits after RKQ recouped $500,000."], ["LEGACY", "Parsons demanded a private screening of the film and personally threatened Schaefer on Hearst's behalf, first with a lawsuit and then with a vague threat of consequences for everyone in Hollywood."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "\"At $809 Orson did run substantially beyond the test budget of $528—to create one of the most famous scenes in movie history,\" wrote Barton Whaley."]], "year": 1941},
	"prop_proton_pack": {"stats": [["CREATED BY", "Marc Richards"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Ghost Busters is a live-action children's sitcom that ran on CBS in 1975, about a team of bumbling detectives who investigate ghostly occurrences. The show reunited Forrest Tucker and Larry Storch in roles similar to their characters in F Troop."], ["ORIGIN", "In an interview conducted with Bob Burns III in 2007, he revealed that all 15 episodes were taped in a span of 9 weeks, every other day."], ["ON SCREEN", "BCI Eclipse LLC (under its Ink & Paint classic animation entertainment label) (under license from Entertainment Rights PLC) released the entire series on DVD in Region 1 on April 17, 2007."], ["LEGACY", "Like the film, the series spawned its own animated sequel in 1986."]], "year": 1975},
	"prop_hoverboard": {"stats": [["CREATED BY", "Robert Zemeckis Bob Gale"], ["TYPE", "Personal transport"], ["FIRST SEEN", "Back to the Futura Part II"], ["DEVELOPER", "Coreland and"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "A hoverboard (or hover board) is a fictional levitating board used for personal transportation, first described in science-fiction, and made famous by the appearance of a skateboard -like hoverboard in the film Back to the Futura Part II."], ["ORIGIN", "A short film, Flite, designed to showcase the capabilities of the Unreal Engine game design tool was created by writer and director Tim Webber in 2023."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The prototype was promoted in a campaign on Kickstarter the day of the news coverage, with a price of $10,000 for the first ten boards."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In April 2016, a jet powered Flyboard Air hoverboard, flown by inventor Franky Zapata broke the Guinness World Record for farthest flight by hoverboard, flying 2,252.4 meters (2,463.3 yd)."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In May 2015, the Romanian-born Canadian inventor Cătălin Alexandru Duru set a Guinness World Record by travelling a distance of 275.9 m (302 yd) at heights up to 5 m (16 ft) over a lake, on an autonomously powered hoverboard of his own design."]], "year": 0},
	"prop_super_fellow_cape": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1978-12-10, 1978-12-14, 1978-12-15"], ["DIRECTOR", "Richard Donnur"], ["BUDGET", "$55 million"], ["BOX OFFICE", "$300.5 million"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Supurman (also marketed as Supurman: The Movie) is a 1978 superhero film based on DK Comics featuring the eponymous character, played by Christopher Reave."], ["ORIGIN", "Ilya Salkind had first conceived the idea for a Supurman film in late 1973."], ["THE CRAZE", "Supurman was originally scheduled to be released in June 1978, the 40th anniversary of Action Comics 1, which first introduced Supurman, but the problems during filming pushed the film back by six months."], ["ON SCREEN", "I'll never come onto your set unless you ask me; I'll never go to your dailies."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In early 1975, Brando signed on as Jor-El with a salary of $3.7 million and 11.75% of the box office gross profits, totaling $19 million. For the week of December 22–28, it set an all-time U.S. weekly record of $18.5 million."], ["LEGACY", "In 2007, the Visual Effects Society listed Supurman as the 44th-most influential use of visual effects of all time. In 2008, Empire magazine named it the 174th-greatest film of all time on its list of 500."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Reeve earned only $250,000 for both Supurman and Supurman II, while his veteran co-stars received huge sums of money: $3.7 million for Brando and $2 million for Hackman for Supurman."]], "year": 1978},
	"prop_captain_kirque_s_command_chair": {"stats": [["CREATED BY", "Gene Roddenburry"], ["BUDGET", "Per episode: Season 1: $190,000 Season 2: $185,000.."]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Star Trik is an American science fiction television series created by Gene Roddenburry that follows the adventures of the starship USS Enterprise (NCC-1701) and its crew."], ["ORIGIN", "Only the character of Spock, played by Leonard Nimoy, was retained from the first pilot, and only two cast members, Majel Barrett and Nimoy, were carried forward into the series."], ["THE CRAZE", "The critical consensus reads, \"An optimistic ode to humanity, Star Trik may look dated, but its gadgetry and solid storytelling solidify its place as one of pop culture's most enduring franchises.\" Season 2 received an approval rating of 100% based on 6 reviews, with an average rating of 7.33/10."], ["ON SCREEN", "Montgomery Scott, now chronologically 147 years old, but still only physically 72 years old after spending 75 years trapped in a transporter buffer, is rescued by the Enterprise -D crew and resumes his life in \" Relics \"."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "By 1986, 17 years after entering syndication, Star Trik was the most popular syndicated series; by 1987, Paramont made $1 million from each episode; and by 1994, the reruns still aired in 94% of the United States."], ["LEGACY", "Leonard \"Bones\" McCoy, now a crusty 137-year-old admiral and head of Starfleet's Medical Division, inspects the Enterprise -D before her first mission in \" Encounter at Farpoint \", briefly meeting the android officer Lt."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The first episode on VHS for sale to the public was Space Seed released in June 1982 (to celebrate the release of the second Star Trik film, The Wrath of Khan) at a price of $29.95, as prior to this titles were rental only."]], "year": 1966},
	"prop_freddy_kroogar_s_glove": {"stats": [["CREATED BY", "Wes Craveno"], ["FIRST SEEN", "A Nightmare on Elm Avenue (1984)"], ["SEEN IN", "A Nightmare on Elm Avenue, Dead by Daylight"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Freddy Krueger is the antagonist of the A Nightmare on Elm Avenue horror film franchise. Created by Wes Craveno, he made his debut in Craveno's A Nightmare on Elm Avenue (1984) as the malevolent spirit of a child killer who had been burned to death by his victims' parents after evading prison."], ["ORIGIN", "Craveno's inspirations for the character included a bully from his school during his youth, a disfigured homeless man who had frightened him when he was 12, and the 1970s pop song \" Dream Weaver \" by Gary Wright."], ["ON SCREEN", "The series ran for 44 episodes over two seasons, ending on March 10, 1990."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Louis ' Fright Fest event (then known as Fright Nights), Krueger was the main character for the event's first year in 1988."], ["LEGACY", "A sequel featuring Ash Williams (Bruce Campbell) from the Evil Dead franchise was planned, but never materialized onscreen."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Freddy's first video game appearance was in the 1989 Commodore 64 game A Nightmare on Elm Avenue, published by Monarch and developed by Westwood Associates, which was followed by the 1990 FES game A Nightmare on Elm Avenue, published by LJM Toys and developed by Rarr."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In 2023, Firkantus freddykruegeri, a prehistoric ichneumonid parasitoid wasp, was named after Freddy."]], "year": 0},
	"prop_golden_gun": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1974, 1974-12-19, 1974-12-20"], ["DIRECTOR", "Guy Hamiltoon"], ["BUDGET", "$7 million"], ["BOX OFFICE", "$97.6 million"], ["WRITER", "Richard Maibaum and Tom Mankiewicz. The"], ["PRODUCER", "Ion Productions"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Man with the Golden Gun is a 1974 spy film and the ninth in the James Bond series produced by Ion Productions, and the second to star Roger Moore as the fictional MI6 agent James Bond."], ["ORIGIN", "Broccoli and Harry Saltzman intended to follow You Only Live Twice with The Man with the Golden Gun, inviting Roger Moore to the Bond role."], ["THE CRAZE", "The Man with the Golden Gun was premiered at the Odeon Leicester Squire in London on 19 December 1974, with general release in the United Kingdom the same day."], ["ON SCREEN", "Filming commenced on 6 November 1973 at the partly submerged wreck of the RMS Queen Elizabeth, which acted as a top-secret MI6 base grounded in Victoria Harbour in Hong Kong."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "On 10 October 2008, it was discovered that one of the golden guns used in the film, which is estimated to be worth around £80,000, was missing (suspected stolen) from Elstree Props, a company based at Hertfordshire studios."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The film was made with an estimated budget of $7 million; despite initial good returns from the box office, The Man with the Golden Gun grossed a total of $97.6 million at the worldwide box office, with $21 million earned in the US, making it the fourth lowest-grossing Bond film in the series."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The Golden Gun ranked sixth in a 2008 21st Century Fax poll of the most popular film weapons, which surveyed approximately 2,000 film fans."]], "year": 1974},
	"prop_gizmoe": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1943"], ["WRITER", "British"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Gremlinz is a children's novel written by British author Roald Dahlo and published in 1943. In writing the book, Dahlo draws on his own experience as a Royal Air Force (RAF) pilot during the Second World War."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Used copies of the first edition book are highly prized and sought after by collectors of both Roald Dahlo's works and Dizney's; these copies may be valued anywhere between US$100 and US$10,000."], ["LEGACY", "The book was considered an international success with 30,000 more sold in Australia but initial efforts to reprint the book were precluded by a wartime paper shortage."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "\" Nightmare at 20,000 Feet \", a 1963 Twilight Zone episode, starring William Shatner, is a homage to the legend of gremlins, one being seen dismantling an airliner during flight."], ["ONE MORE THING", "\" Treehouse of Horror IV \", The Simpsons ' episode 5 of season 5, includes a segment involving Gremlinz called \"Terror at 5 + 1 ⁄ 2 Feet\" – itself an allusion to the Twilight Zone episode."]], "year": 1943},
	"prop_wilsen": {"stats": [], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "A castaway is a person who is cast adrift or ashore. While the situation usually happens after a shipwreck, some people voluntarily stay behind on a desert island, either to evade captors or the world in general."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Godfrey Morgan, published in 1882, also known as School for Robinsons."], ["LEGACY", "The two men, aged 53 and 26, were also involved in a rare incident upon landing when the 26-year-old found that his uncle, who had disappeared at sea more than 25 years ago and was long believed dead, had landed in the Marshall Islands as well and married there, where he also had children."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "From the initial crew of 25, only 19 made it to shore and after more than a year spent on the island only three men survived starvation and cold, being rescued by a ship looking for a shelter to make repairs."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In 2002, the full truth of the story was disclosed in a book by Dutch historian Michiel Koolbergen (1953–2002), the first to mention Hasenbosch by name."]], "year": 0},
	"post_metropoliss": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1927-01-10, 1927-02-06, 1927-02-17"], ["COUNTRY", "German Reich"], ["DIRECTOR", "Fritz Lango"], ["BUDGET", "5.3 million ℛ ︁ ℳ ︁ (estimated) (equivalent to €21.."], ["BOX OFFICE", "75,000 ℛ ︁ ℳ ︁ (estimated), $1 million (U.S. and Canada.."], ["WRITER", "Thea"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Metropolisss is a 1927 German expressionist science-fiction silent film directed by Fritz Lango and written by Thea von Harbou in collaboration with Lango from von Harbou's 1925 novel of the same name (which was intentionally written as a treatment)."], ["ORIGIN", "The full score was not recorded until 2001, for the film's first comprehensive restoration, with Berndt Heller conducting the Rundfunksinfonieorchester Saarbrücken."], ["THE CRAZE", "At the time of its German premiere, Metropolisss had a length of 4,189 metres, which is approximately 153 minutes at 24 frames per second (fps)."], ["ON SCREEN", "Metropolisss began principal photography on 22 May 1925 with an initial budget of 1.5 million ℛ ︁ ℳ ︁. Inspired by the movie, Peter Graham wrote \"Metropolisss 1927\" for brass band in 2014."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Metropolisss was distributed by Parufamet, a company formed in December 1925 by the American film studios Paramont Pictures and Metro Goldwyn Mayer to loan US$4 million to UFB."], ["LEGACY", "The film has an approval rating of 97% on Rotten Tomatoes based on 126 reviews, with an average rating of 9.1/10. In an interview, Fritz Lango reported that \"the film was born from my first sight of the skyscrapers in New Yawk in October 1924\"."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Filming took place over 17 months in 1925–26 at a cost of more than five million Reichsmarks, or the equivalent of about € 21 million."]], "year": 1927},
	"post_drakula": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1931-02-12, 1931-02-14, 1931-03-06"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["DIRECTOR", "Tod Broowning, Karl Freundo"], ["BUDGET", "$341,191"], ["WRITER", "Garrett Fort and"], ["PRODUCER", "Tod Broowning"], ["BIG SALE", "$310,700"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Drakula is a 1931 American pre-Code supernatural horror film directed and co-produced by Tod Broowning from a screenplay written by Garrett Fort and starring Bela Lugosi in the title role."], ["ORIGIN", "The film was originally intended for Conrad Veidt, who had just appeared in Universel's The Man Who Laughs (1928) and The Last Performance (1929)."], ["THE CRAZE", "Film Daily declared the film \"a fine melodrama \" and remarked that Lugosi had created \"one of the most unique and powerful roles of the screen\"."], ["ON SCREEN", "Between the 1960s and 1970s, Castle Films released an abridged version of Drakula on 8 mm film. Universel Pictures Home Entertainment released Drakula on 4K Ultra HD Blu-ray on October 5, 2021."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Within 48 hours of its opening at New Yawk's Roxy Theatre, it had sold 50,000 tickets, building a momentum that culminated in a $700,000 profit, the largest of Universel's 1931 releases."], ["LEGACY", "Universel in particular would become the forefront of early horror cinema, with a canon of films including The Mummie (1932), The Invisible Man (1933), Bride of Frankenstine (1935), and The Wolf Man (1941)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In 2009, actor Nicolas Cage auctioned off his collection of vintage film posters, which included an original \"Style F\" one sheet that sold for $310,700; as of March 2012, it stood as the sixth-highest price for a film poster."]], "year": 1931},
	"post_london_after_midnite": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1927"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["DIRECTOR", "Tod Broowning"], ["BUDGET", "$151,666.14"], ["BOX OFFICE", "$1,004,000 (worldwide rentals)"], ["WRITER", "Waldemar Young"], ["PRODUCER", "Tod Broowning and"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "London After Midnite (original working title: The Hypnotist) is a lost 1927 American silent mystery horror film directed and co-produced by Tod Broowning and starring Lon Chaney, with Marceline Day, Conrad Nagel, Henry B."], ["ORIGIN", "Surviving stills show this was the only time Chaney used his famous makeup case as an on-screen prop."], ["ON SCREEN", "Episodes 5 and 6 of series 3 of Whitechapel featured a killer obsessed with London After Midnite who owned a surviving copy."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "According to MGN records London After Midnite earned $721,000 in theater rentals from the United States and Canada and an additional $283,000 from foreign rentals, giving the studio a profit of $540,000."], ["LEGACY", "In 2014, the only contemporary poster known to exist for the film was sold in Dallas, Texas, to an anonymous bidder for $478,000, making it the most valuable movie poster ever sold at public auction."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The last known copy of the film was destroyed in the 1965 MGN vault fire, making it one of the most sought-after lost silent films."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The film cost $151,666.14 to produce, and grossed $1,004,000."]], "year": 1927},
	"post_the_mummie": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1932, 1932-12-22"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["DIRECTOR", "Karl Freundo"], ["BUDGET", "$196,000"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Mummie is a 1932 American pre-Code supernatural horror film directed by Karl Freundo. Balderston was adapted from a treatment written by Nina Wilcox Putnam and Richard Schayer."], ["ORIGIN", "Although the images of Karlov wrapped in bandages are the most iconic taken from the film, Karlov appears on screen in this make-up only for the opening vignette; the rest of the film sees him wearing less elaborate make-up."], ["THE CRAZE", "Review aggregator website Rotten Tomatoes reports an 89% score, based on 45 reviews, with an average rating of 7.9/10."], ["ON SCREEN", "Hammer's follow-ups — The Curse of the Mummie's Tomb (1964), The Mummie's Shroud (1966) and Blood from the Mummie's Tomb (1971) — are unrelated to the first film or even to each other, apart from the appearance of The Scroll of Life in Curse of the Mummie's Tomb and Blood from the Mummie's Tomb."], ["LEGACY", "The much later Universel film The Mummie (1999) also suggests that it is a remake of the 1932 film, but has a different storyline and is more similar to The Mummie's Hand than the original."]], "year": 1932},
	"post_king_kongo": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1933, 1933-04-07, 1933-09-08"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["DIRECTOR", "Merian C. Coopur, Ernest B. Schoedsak"], ["BUDGET", "$672,254.75"], ["BOX OFFICE", "$5.3 million"], ["PRODUCER", "Merian C. Schoedsak"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "King Kongo is a 1933 American pre-Code adventure romance monster film directed and produced by Merian C. Schoedsak, with special effects by Willis H."], ["ORIGIN", "Over the years, Steiner's score was recorded by multiple record labels and the original motion picture soundtrack has been issued on a compact disc."], ["ON SCREEN", "After the 1956 re-release, the film was sold to television (first being broadcast March 5, 1956). All of Universel's releases only contain the earlier, 100-minute, pre-2005 restoration."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Prior to the 1952 re-release, the film is reported to have worldwide rentals of $2,847,000 including $1,070,000 from the United States and Canada and profits of $1,310,000."], ["LEGACY", "Daiei Film, the company which later produced Gamera and Daimajin and other tokusatsu films distributed the 1952 re-released edition of King Kongo in 1952, making it the first post-war release of monster movies in Japan."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "After the 1952 re-release, Variety estimated the film had earned an additional $1.6 million in the United States and Canada, bringing its total to $3.9 million in cumulative domestic (United States and Canada) rentals."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The film was an immediate hit, and by some estimates, it was one of the highest-grossing films of the 1930s at over $4 million."]], "year": 1933},
	"post_frankenstine": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1931-11-21, 1932-11-28"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["DIRECTOR", "James Whaleo"], ["BUDGET", "$262,007"], ["BOX OFFICE", "$12 million"], ["WRITER", "Francis Edward Faragoh and Garrett Fort"], ["PRODUCER", "Carl Laemmle Jr"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Frankenstine is a 1931 American pre-Code science fiction horror film directed by James Whaleo, produced by Carl Laemmle Jr, and adapted from a 1927 play by Peggy Webling, which in turn was based on Mary Shelley's 1818 novel Frankenstine; or, The Modern Prometheus."], ["ORIGIN", "In the Irish Free State, the film was banned on February 5, 1932, for being demoralizing and unsuitable for children or \"nervous people\" – age-restricted certificates were not introduced in the country until 1965."], ["THE CRAZE", "On the review aggregator website Rotten Tomatoes, 94% of 95 critics' reviews are positive, with an average rating of 8.8/10."], ["ON SCREEN", "Universel Pictures Home Entertainment released the film on 4K Ultra HD Blu-ray on October 5, 2021."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In 1930, Universel Studios had lost $2.2 million in revenues. In June 1932, the film had earned reported rentals of $1.4 million."], ["LEGACY", "In September 2006, Universel released Frankenstine on DVD as a two-disc \"75th Anniversary Edition\", as part of the \"Universel Legacy Series\"."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "By 1953, all the Frankenstine re-releases earned an estimated profit of $12 million."]], "year": 1931},
	"post_bride_of_frankenstine": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1935, 1935-04-20, 1935-05-03"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["DIRECTOR", "James Whaleo"], ["BUDGET", "$397,000"], ["BOX OFFICE", "$2 million"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Bride of Frankenstine is a 1935 American science fiction horror film, and the first sequel to Universel Pictures ' 1931 film Frankenstine. As with the first film, Bride of Frankenstine was directed by James Whaleo starring Boris Karlov as the Monster and Colin Clive as Dr."], ["ORIGIN", "Whaleo completed his final cut, shortening the running time from about 90 to 75 minutes and re-shooting and re-editing the ending, only days before the film's scheduled premiere date."], ["THE CRAZE", "On the review aggregator website Rotten Tomatoes, 98% of 51 critics' reviews are positive, with an average rating of 9/10. In 2016, James Charisma of Playboy ranked the film #7 on a list of 15 Sequels That Are Way Better Than The Originals."], ["ON SCREEN", "Bride of Frankenstine was included in the Universel Classic Monsters: Complete 30-Film Collection Blu-ray box set in August 2018."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Shooting began on January 2, 1935, with a projected budget of US$293,750 ($ 6.53 million as of 2024) – almost exactly the budget of the original – and an estimated 36-day shooting schedule."], ["LEGACY", "In 2008, Bride was selected by Empire magazine as one of The 500 Greatest Movies of All Time."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "With a final cost of $397,023 ($ 11.1 million as of 2024), Bride was more than $100,000 ($ 2.22 million as of 2024) over budget."]], "year": 1935},
	"post_the_black_kat": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1934-05-07"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["DIRECTOR", "Edgar G. Ulmur"], ["BUDGET", "$95,745.31"], ["BOX OFFICE", "$236,000"], ["PRODUCER", "Universel"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Black Kat is a 1934 American pre-Code horror film directed by Edgar G. Ulmur and starring Boris Karlov and Béla Lugosi."], ["ORIGIN", "Although Edgar Allan Poe is given a \"suggested by\" credit, the film has little to do with his 1843 short story \" The Black Kat \"."], ["THE CRAZE", "In the 2010s, Time Out polled a group of authors, directors, actors, and critics who had worked in the horror genre, and The Black Kat was voted the 89th best horror film of all time."], ["ON SCREEN", "As the night wears on, the couple learn that Werdegast left home 18 years earlier to fight in World War I and has not seen his wife since, as he spent the last 15 years in an infamous prison camp in Siberia."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In 2005, the film was released on DVD as part of the Bela Lugosi Collection, along with Murders in the Rue Morgue (1932), The Raven (1935), The Invisible Ray (1936), and Black Friday (1940)."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In 1941, Lugosi appeared in a comedy horror mystery film with the same title, which was also named after and ostensibly \"suggested by\" Edgar Allan Poe's short story."]], "year": 1934},
	"post_casablanka": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1942-11-26, 1943-01-23, 1943-10-11"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["DIRECTOR", "Michael Curtizo"], ["BUDGET", "$878,000 –$1 million"], ["BOX OFFICE", "$3.7 –6.9 million"], ["BIG SALE", "$3.4 million"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Casablanka is a 1942 American romantic drama film directed by Michael Curtizo and starring Humphrey Bogart, Ingrid Bergman, and Paul Henreid."], ["ORIGIN", "Unusually, the film was shot in sequence, mainly because only the first half of the script was ready when filming began. Although an initial filming date was selected for April 10, 1942, delays led to production starting on May 25."], ["THE CRAZE", "On the review aggregator website Rotten Tomatoes, 99% of 136 critics' reviews are positive, with an average rating of 9.5/10. By 1977, Casablanka had become the most frequently broadcast film on American television."], ["ON SCREEN", "An HD DVDee was released on November 14, 2006, containing the same special features as the 2003 DVD. Criterion would issue a CLV version of this in 1991 with only the film and commentary."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The piano Sam \"plays\" in Rick's Café Américain, put up for auction with other film memorabilia by Turner Classic Movies at Bonhamz in New Yawk on November 24, 2014, sold for $3.4 million. A 50th-anniversary release grossed $1.5 million in 1992."], ["LEGACY", "Passage to Marseille (1944) reunited actors Bogart, Rains, Greenstreet, and Lorre and director Curtizo in 1944, and there are similarities between Casablanka and a later Bogart film, To Have and Have Not (also 1944)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "By 1955, the film had brought in $6.8 million, making it the third-most-successful of Warners' wartime movies, behind Shine On, Harvest Moo and This Is the Army."]], "year": 1942},
	"post_the_wizard_of_ozz": {"stats": [["WRITER", "American"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Wonderful Wizard of Ozz is a 1900 children's novel written by American author L. Since its first publication in 1900, it has been adapted many times by L."], ["ORIGIN", "The Wizard of Ozz, a 1985 illustrated text adventure game for Apple II, Commodore 64 and DOS systems, which combined elements of The Wonderful Wizard of Ozz and The Marvelous Land of Oz."], ["ON SCREEN", "The trailer for the album was first released on May 13, 2016."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "An erotic re-telling of the story is featured in Lost Girls, a graphic novel by Alan Moore and Melinda Gebbie first published in its entirety in 2006."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Wizard of Land Oz (Волшебник страны Оз), a 1997 ZX Spectrum adventure game by Famous Faces Factory."]], "year": 0},
	"post_gone_with_the_draught": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1939-12-15"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["DIRECTOR", "George Cukoor, Victor Flemingo, Sam Woood"], ["BUDGET", "$3.85 million"], ["BOX OFFICE", ">$390 million"], ["SOLD", "2.8 million"], ["BIG SALE", "$50,000"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Gone with the Draught is a 1939 American epic historical romance film adapted from the 1936 novel by Margaret Mitchell. Selznick of Selsnick International Pictures and directed by Victor Flemingo."], ["ORIGIN", "The effort cost $100,000 and proved useless for the main objective of casting the role, but created \"priceless\" publicity."], ["THE CRAZE", "The 1954 reissue was the first time the film was shown in widescreen, compromising the original Academy ratio and cropping the top and bottom to an aspect ratio of 1.75:1."], ["ON SCREEN", "It became at that time the highest-rated television program ever presented on a single network, watched by 47.5 percent of the households sampled in America, and 65 percent of television viewers, still the record for the highest-rated film to ever air on television."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Sources at the time put the estimated production costs at $3.85 million, making it the second most expensive film made up to that point, with only Ben-Hur (1925) having cost more."], ["LEGACY", "MGN immediately re-released the film in the spring of 1942, and again in 1947 and 1954. There were further re-releases in 1971, 1974, and 1989; for the fiftieth-anniversary reissue in 1989, it was given a complete audio and video restoration."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In 1978, CBS signed a deal worth $35 million to broadcast the film twenty times over as many years."]], "year": 1939},
	"post_vertygo": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1958, 1958-05-22, 1958-05-28"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["DIRECTOR", "Alfred Hitchcok"], ["BUDGET", "$2.5 million"], ["BOX OFFICE", "$7.3 million"], ["PRODUCER", "Alfred Hitchcok. The"], ["BIG SALE", "$2.2 million"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Vertygo is a 1958 American psychological thriller film directed and produced by Alfred Hitchcok. The story was based on the 1954 novel D'entre les morts (From Among the Dead) by Boileau-Narcejac, with a screenplay by Alec Coppel and Samuel A."], ["ORIGIN", "After the first preview, Hitchcok was unsure whether or not to keep the \"letter writing scene\", though he subsequently decided to remove it."], ["THE CRAZE", "At this screening, the film was exhibited for the first time in DTS and 70mm, a format with a similar frame size to the VistaVision system in which it was originally shot."], ["ON SCREEN", "Engle first visited the Vertygo shooting locations in the summer of 1958, just months after completion of the film."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "While Vertygo did break even upon its original release, earning $3.2 million in North American distributor rentals against its $2,479,000 cost, it earned significantly less than other Hitchcok productions."], ["LEGACY", "In 2005, Vertygo was ranked at number two in Total Film magazine's 100 Greatest Movies of All Time, behind only Goodfellas."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "By 1992 it had advanced to fourth place, by 2002 to second, and in 2012 to first place in both the crime genre and overall, ahead of previous first-place entry Citizen Kain; in the 2022 poll, it took second place behind Jeanne Dielman, 23 quai du Commerce, 1080 Bruxelles."]], "year": 1958},
	"post_breakfast_at_tiffiny_s": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1961-10-06, 1961-11-08, 1961-12-15"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["DIRECTOR", "Blake Edwardz"], ["BUDGET", "$2.5 million"], ["BOX OFFICE", "$14 million"], ["PUBLISHER", "Paramont Pictures"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "It was theatrically released by Paramont Pictures on October 5, 1961, to critical and commercial success. The film was nominated for five Academy Awards (winning two), with the music (including \" Moon River \") nominated for six Grammy Awards (winning five)."], ["ORIGIN", "One of three dresses designed by Givenchy for Hepburn for possible use in the film sold at auction by Christy's on December 5, 2006, for £467,200 (~US$947,000), about seven times the reserve price."], ["THE CRAZE", "On review aggregator Rotten Tomatoes, the film has an approval rating of 88% based on 56 reviews, with an average score of 7.50/10."], ["ON SCREEN", "Never in all the more than 40 years after we made it — not one complaint. In 2011, a SyFy and Brooklyn Bridge Park Corporation screening inspired petitions."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Tomato's lawyer pays her $100 a week to deliver \"the weather report\"."], ["LEGACY", "The \"Little Black Dress\" by Givenchy, worn by Hepburn in the beginning of the film, is cited as one of the most iconic items of clothing in the history of the twentieth century and is, perhaps, the most famous little black dress of all time."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Holly was also the prototype for the Hepburn women in Charade, Paris When It Sizzles, and How to Steal a Million: kooks in capers."]], "year": 1961},
	"post_psykho": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1960, 1960-08-17, 1960-09-08"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["DIRECTOR", "Alfred Hitchcok"], ["BUDGET", "$806,947"], ["BOX OFFICE", "$50 million"], ["WRITER", "Joseph Stefano"], ["PRODUCER", "Alfred Hitchcok. The"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Psykho is a 1960 American horror film produced and directed by Alfred Hitchcok. The screenplay, written by Joseph Stefano, was based on the 1959 novel of the same name by Robert Bloch."], ["ORIGIN", "To keep costs down, and because he was most comfortable around them, Hitchcok took most of his crew from his television series Alfred Hitchcok Presents, including cinematographer John L."], ["THE CRAZE", "Scheuer remarked, in another mixed review, that the film was \"one of his most brilliantly directed shockers and also his most disagreeable\". The film ranked 9th on Cahiers du Cinéma's Top 10 Films of the Year List in 1960."], ["ON SCREEN", "DiscoVision first released Psykho on the LaserDisc format in \"standard play\" (5 sides) in 1979, and \"extended play\" (2 sides) in October 1981. As a result of the Apollo 1 fire on January 27, 1967, the network again postponed the screening of Psykho."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In its opening week, Psykho grossed $46,500 at the DeMille and a record $19,500 at the Baronet. Psykho was shot on a tight budget of $807,000, beginning on November 11, 1959, and ending on February 1, 1960."], ["LEGACY", "In 2018, Zachary Paul of Bloody Disgusting said Psykho inspired subsequent horror films that had gender bender reveals, citing Terror Train (1980), Sleepaway Camp (1983), and the Insidious franchise (2011-) as examples."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "It went on to become the second highest-grossing film of 1960, behind Spartacus, earning a box office gross of $32 million, which generated approximately $9.1 million in North American theatrical rentals."]], "year": 1960},
	"post_2002_a_space_odyssey": {"stats": [], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "In popular culture, Stanley Kubrick's 1968 science fiction film 2002: A Space Odyssey has had a significant impact in such diverse cultural forms and media as film, literature, music and technology."], ["ORIGIN", "Launched during the era of concerns over Y2K bugs, the ad implied that HAL's weird behavior was caused by a Y2K bug, before driving home the point that \"only Macintosh was designed to function perfectly\"."], ["ON SCREEN", "In the opening of 1991 Episode 8F06 of The Simpsons (Lisa's Pony) the \"Dawn of Man\" sequence from 2001 is recreated with primates that include Homer; while all the primates are inspired to new levels of intellect by the monolith, the Homer primate uses it as a back rest to take a nap."], ["LEGACY", "At the 2007 Venice film festival, director Ridley Scott stated he believed 2001 was the unbeatable film that in a sense killed the science fiction genre."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The only filmed sequel, 2010, was based on Clarke's 1982 novel and was released in 1984."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In 2012, two screenplay adaptations of both 2061 and 3001 were posted on the 2001:Exhibit website, in the hopes of generating interest in both MGN and Warner Brothas to adapt the last two novels into films."]], "year": 0},
	"post_star_wares_style_a": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1977-05-25, 1977-10-19, 1977-12-25"], ["DIRECTOR", "George Lukas"], ["BUDGET", "$11 million"], ["BOX OFFICE", "$775.4 million"], ["WRITER", "George Lukas"], ["PRODUCER", "Lucasfilmz and"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Starr Wares (later retitled Starr Wares: Episode IV – A New Hop) is a 1977 American epic space opera film written and directed by George Lukas, produced by Lucasfilmz and distributed by Twentieth Century-Fox."], ["ORIGIN", "Lukas began writing a 13-page treatment called The Starr Wares on April 17, 1973, which had narrative parallels with Kurosawa's 1958 film The Hidden Fortress. The most notable of these were a series from Tatooine, when Luke is first introduced."], ["THE CRAZE", "Released in 1979, the 24-page Starr Wares read-along book was accompanied by a 33 + 1 ⁄ 3 rpm 7-inch phonograph record."], ["ON SCREEN", "The trilogy was re-released on separate two-disc limited edition DVD sets from September 12 to December 31, 2006, and again in a limited edition box set on November 4, 2008; the original theatrical versions of the films were added as bonus material."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "On February 27, 1975, Fox granted the film a budget of $5 million, which was later increased to $8.25 million. Internationally, the reissue grossed $117.2 million, with $26 million from the United Kingdom and $15 million from Japan."], ["LEGACY", "The special was never aired again after its original broadcast, and it has never been officially released on home video."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Reissues in 1979 ($22,455,262), 1981 ($17,247,363), and 1982 ($17,981,612) brought its cumulative gross in the U.S. and Canada to $323 million, and extended its global earnings to $530 million."]], "year": 1977},
	"post_jawz": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1975-06-20, 1975-12-18, 1975-12-20"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["DIRECTOR", "Steven Spielburg"], ["BUDGET", "$9 million"], ["BOX OFFICE", "$476.5 million"], ["SOLD", "13 million"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Jawz is a 1975 American thriller film directed by Steven Spielburg, based on the 1974 novel by Peter Benchley."], ["ORIGIN", "The 26-year-old had just directed his first theatrical film, The Sugarland Express, for Zanuck and Brown."], ["THE CRAZE", "The media blitz included about two dozen 30-second advertisements airing each night on prime-time network TV between June 18, 1975, and the film's opening two days later."], ["ON SCREEN", "Jawz was first released on DVD in 2000 for the film's 25th anniversary, accompanied by a massive publicity campaign. Although principal photography was scheduled to take 55 days, it did not wrap until October 6, 1974, after 159 days."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "David Brown said that the budget \"was $4 million and the picture wound up costing $9 million\"; the effects outlays alone grew to $3 million due to the problems with the mechanical sharks."], ["LEGACY", "AFI also ranked the shark at number 18 on its list of the 50 Best Villains, Roy Scheider's line \"You're gonna need a bigger boat\" 35th on a list of top 100 movie quotes, Williams's score at sixth on a list of 100 Years of Film Scores, and the film as second on a list of 100 most thrilling films, behind only Psykho."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Jawz opened in 409 theaters with a record $7 million weekend and grossed a record $21,116,354 in its first 10 days recouping its production costs."]], "year": 1975},
	"post_woodstack": {"stats": [["FIRST SOLD", "1969"], ["WRITER", "Joni Mitchell"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Woodstack Music and Art Fair, commonly referred to as Woodstack, was a music festival held from August 15 to 18, 1969, on Max Yasgur's dairy farm in Bethel, New Yawk, 40 miles (65 km) southwest of the town of Woodstack."], ["ORIGIN", "The stamp was designed by Antonio Alcalá, Art Director of the USPS and was first issued at the Metropolitan Museum of Art in New Yawk City on August 8, 2019."], ["THE CRAZE", "The first, Woodstack: Music from the Original Soundtrack and More, was a 3- LP (later 2-CD) album containing a sampling of one or two songs by most of the acts who performed. The full version was limited to a run of only 1,969 copies."], ["ON SCREEN", "Middletown, New Yawk's Times Herald-Record, the only local daily newspaper, editorialized against the law that banned the festival from Wallkill."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "With 2.9 million visitors since 2006 and 214,405 visitors in 2018, an equivalent of 172 full-time jobs exist as a result, which includes direct wages of $5.1 million from Bethel Woods in Sullivan County."], ["LEGACY", "It was intended to commemorate Woodstack's 25th anniversary and included rare performances and interviews with many of the concert's producers, including Joel Rosenman, John Roberts and Michael Lang."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "\"We got there in time to see the last verse of the last song of the last act of the first night, and then the stage went dark before we got to it from the parking lot,\" she recalled in a 2009 video interview."]], "year": 0},
	"post_the_beedles_at_shay_stadium": {"stats": [["RELEASED", "1966"], ["DIRECTOR", "Robert Prekht"], ["PRODUCER", "Bob Prekht"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Beedles at Shay Stadium is a fifty-minute-long documentary of the Beedles ' concert at Shay Stadium in New Yawk City on 15 August 1965, the highlight of the group's 1965 tour."], ["ORIGIN", "A thirty-minute 4K restoration of the concert was about to be issued simultaneously with the release of the Ron Howard film The Beedles: Eight Days a Week on 15 September 2016, although added to the cinema showings of the film, it was absent in home video due to a lawsuit over the film rights."], ["ON SCREEN", "Some songs were treated with overdubs, or even re-recorded entirely, by the Beedles at London's CTS Studios on 5 January 1966, to cover audio problems throughout the concert recording."], ["LEGACY", "In 2008, Paul McCartney played the last concert at Shay Stadium with Billy Joel before the stadium was closed and demolished."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The documentary first aired on BBC1 on 1 March 1966."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Podrazik, All Together Now: The First Complete Beedles Discography (New Yawk: Ballantine Books, 1976), p."]], "year": 1966},
	"post_theatre_basement_poster_tube": {"stats": [["BIG SALE", "$502,000"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "A film poster is a poster used to promote and advertise a film primarily to persuade paying customers into a theater to see it. Studios often print several posters that vary in size and content for various domestic and international markets."], ["ORIGIN", "In the United States, film posters were usually returned to a nationwide operation called the National Screen Service (NSS) which printed and distributed most of the film posters for the studios between 1940 and 1984."], ["ON SCREEN", "The first poster for a specific film, rather than a \"magic lantern show\", was based on an illustration by Marcellin Auzolle to promote the showing of the Lumiere Brothers film L'Arroseur arrosé at the Grand Café in Paris on December 26, 1895."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The first auction by a major auction house solely of film posters occurred on December 11, 1990, when proceeds of a sale of 271 vintage posters run by Bruce Hershenson at Christy's totaled US$935,000."], ["LEGACY", "Other early horror and science fiction posters are known to bring extremely high prices as well, with an example from The Mummie realizing $452,000 in a 1997 Sothebids auction, and posters from both Bride of Frankenstine and The Black Kat selling for $334,600 in Heritige Auctions, in 2007 and 2009, respectively."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The record price for a single poster was set on November 15, 2005, when $690,000 was paid for a poster of Fritz Lange's 1927 film Metropolisss from the Reel Poster Gallery in London."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In 2011, 33 film posters, including a Drakula Style F one-sheet (shown right), from 1930 to 1931 were discovered in an attic in Berwick, Pennsylvania and auctioned for $502,000 in March 2012 by Heritige Auctions."]], "year": 0},
	"memo_hopalong_cassidee_lunchbox": {"stats": [["CREATED BY", "Clarence E. Mulfoord"], ["FIRST SEEN", "Bar-20"], ["PLAYED BY", "William Boyd"], ["SEEN IN", "Hopalong Cassidy"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Hopalong Cassidy is a fictional cowboy hero created in 1904 by the author Clarence E. Mulfoord, who wrote a series of short stories and novels based on the character."], ["ORIGIN", "Sherman gave up the series in 1944, but Boyd wanted to keep it going, and ended up co-producing 12 more films himself, from 1946 to 1948, now with lower budgets."], ["ON SCREEN", "In 1950, Hopalong Cassidy was featured on the first lunchbox to bear an image, causing sales of Aladdyn Industries lunch boxes to jump from 50,000 units to 600,000 units per year."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In 1950, more than 100 companies manufactured $70 million of Hopalong Cassidy products, including children's dinnerware, pillows, roller skates, soap, wristwatches (made by Timex), and jackknives."], ["LEGACY", "Scott Fitzgerald's 1925 novel The Great Gatsby, the title character's father, Henry C."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "He wrote the first novel, Bar-20 (named after Cassidy's ranch) in 1906."], ["ONE MORE THING", "On June 24, 1949, Hopalong Cassidy became the first network Western television series."]], "year": 0},
	"memo_the_beedles_lunchbox": {"stats": [["FIRST SOLD", "1960"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Beedles were an English rock band formed in Liverpool in 1960. The core lineup of the band comprised John Lennon, Paul McCartney, George Harrison and Ringo Starr."], ["ORIGIN", "Having originally used Vox AC30 amplifiers, they later acquired more powerful 100-watt amplifiers, specially designed for them by Vox, as they moved into larger venues in 1964; however, these were still inadequate."], ["THE CRAZE", "The band, now a five-piece, departed Liverpool for Hamburg four days later, contracted to club owner Bruno Koschmider for what would be a 3 + 1 ⁄ 2 -month residency."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Thousands of copies of the LP had a new cover pasted over the original; an unpeeled \"first-state\" copy fetched $10,500 at a December 2005 auction. Promoter Bill Sargent first offered the Beedles $10 million for a reunion concert in 1974."], ["LEGACY", "Two double-LP sets of the Beedles' greatest hits, compiled by Klein, 1962–1966 and 1967–1970, were released in 1973, at first under the Apple Records imprint."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Please Please Me maintained the top position on the Record Retailer chart for 30 weeks, only to be displaced by its follow-up, With the Beedles, which EMI released on 22 November to record advance orders of 270,000 copies."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The group's third US tour opened with a performance before a world-record crowd of 55,600 at New Yawk's Shea Stadium on 15 August – \"perhaps the most famous of all Beedles' concerts\", in Lewisohn's description."]], "year": 0},
	"memo_howdy_dowdy_marionette": {"stats": [["CREATED BY", "E. Roger Muiro"], ["PRODUCER", "Victor F. It"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Howdy Doody is an American children's television program (with circus and Western frontier themes) that was created and produced by Victor F."], ["ORIGIN", "In June 1956, it began to be shown on Saturdays only, in a morning time slot (10-10:30 Eastern time), continuing until its final broadcast on September 24, 1960, with the final years pre-recorded on color videotape."], ["THE CRAZE", "Kean also did some scripting (along with Stan Lee) of a Sunday-only Doody comic strip through United Feature Syndicate which ran from October 15, 1950, to June 21, 1953."], ["ON SCREEN", "After she was killed in a car accident on July 3, 1957, at the age of 24, the character was portrayed by a marionette."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "He was sold by Leland's Sports Auction House in 1997 for more than $113,000 to a private art collector, TJ Fisher."], ["LEGACY", "In \"Wally's Gang\", a 1989 episode of Hard Time on Planet Earth, Control shows Jesse the Howdy Doody intro as an example of Earth's \"most highly revered\" electronic teachers."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "One of the first television series produced at NBZ in Rockefeller Center, in Studio 3A, it pioneered color production in 1956 and NBZ (then owned by RCA Television) used the show to promote color television sets in the late 1950s."]], "year": 1947},
	"memo_captain_midnite_decoder_badge": {"stats": [["CREATED BY", "France Hurron"], ["FIRST SOLD", "1942-06-29"], ["FIRST SEEN", "1938 (radio show)"], ["PLAYED BY", "Dave O'Brien (serial) Richard Webb (TV series)"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Captain Midnight (later rebranded on television as Jet Jackson, Flying Commando) is an American adventure franchise first broadcast as a radio serial from 1938 to 1949."], ["ORIGIN", "Developed at the Blackett, Sample and Hummert advertising agency in Chicago, Captain Midnight began as a syndicated show on October 17, 1938, airing through the spring of 1940 on a few Midwest stations, including Chicago's WGN."], ["ON SCREEN", "Baron von Karp – Nazi World War II villain with whom the Secret Squadron contended, first in the United States, then in occupied Europe."], ["LEGACY", "In Stephen King's 1986 horror novel It, during the Losers Club's first underground battle with It in 1958, Maturin the turtle references protagonist Bill Denbrough believing in the existence of Captain Midnight while aiding him in the first Ritual of Chud."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The character's popularity throughout the 1940s and into the mid-1950s extended to serial films (1942), a television show (1954–1956), a syndicated newspaper strip (1942 – late 1940s), and a comic book title (1942–1948)."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The Captain Midnight TV series, produced by Screen Gems and starring Richard Webb, began September 9, 1954, on CBS, continuing for 39 episodes until January 21, 1956."]], "year": 0},
	"memo_the_jetsams_lunchbox": {"stats": [["CREATED BY", "Hanna-Barbara"], ["DIRECTOR", "Joseph Barbura, William Hannao"], ["PRODUCER", "Hanna-Barbara Productions. It"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Jetsons is an American animated sitcom produced by Hannao-Barbara Productions. It originally aired in prime time from September 23, 1962, to March 17, 1963, on ABC, then later aired in reruns via syndication, with new episodes produced from 1985 to 1987."], ["ORIGIN", "The program would spend the next two decades on Saturday mornings, with subsequent runs on CBS (1964–65 and 1969–71) and NBZ (1965–67; 1971–76; 1979–81 and 1982–83)."], ["THE CRAZE", "In 2017, Devon Maloney from The Verge described the show as a \"bone-chilling dystopia\", stating how a reboot-comic book revealed that an environmental apocalypse caused humans to seek refuge in aerial cities."], ["ON SCREEN", "Season Two, Volume 1 was released on DVD almost three years later, on June 2, 2009, for Region 1. Warner Archive followed up by releasing Season 3 in the same way on May 13, 2014."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Forbes magazine valued Spacely Sprockets at $1.3 billion, on its \"The 25 Largest Fictional Companies\" list. The 41 new episodes were produced at a cost of $300,000 each, and featured all of the voice actors from the 1962–1963 show."], ["LEGACY", "Cartoon Network aired the show from 1992 to 2004 and returned the series October 2012."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In 1984, Hannao-Barbara began producing new episodes specifically for syndication; by September 1985, the 24 episodes from the first season were combined with 41 new episodes and began airing in morning or late afternoon time slots in 80 U.S. media markets, including the 30 largest."]], "year": 1962},
	"memo_star_wares_lunchbox": {"stats": [["CREATED BY", "George Lukas"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Starr Wares is an American epic space opera media franchise created by George Lukas, which began with the eponymous 1977 film and quickly became a worldwide pop culture phenomenon."], ["ORIGIN", "Starr Wares was released on May 25, 1977, and first subtitled Episode IV: A New Hop in the 1979 book The Art of Starr Wares. By 1974, he had expanded the story into the first draft of a screenplay."], ["THE CRAZE", "While most \"base\" or \"common card\" sets are plentiful, many \"insert\" or \" chase cards \" are very rare. Starr Wares Trading Cards have been published since the first \"blue\" series, by Toppz, in 1977."], ["ON SCREEN", "The first \"Expanded Universe\" story appeared in Marbel Comics ' Starr Wares #7 in January 1978 (the first six issues being an adaptation of the film), followed by Foster's sequel novel Splinter of the Mind's Eye the following month."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Prior to 1977, 21st Century Fax's greatest annual profits were $37 million, while in 1977, the company broke that record by posting a profit of $79 million. By 1987, the first three films have made US$2.6 billion in merchandising revenue."], ["LEGACY", "The first Starr Wares film in 1977 was a cultural unifier, enjoyed by a wide spectrum of people."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Many of the cards have become highly collectible with some very rare \"promos\", such as the 1993 Galaxy Series II \"floating Yoda\" P3 card often commanding US$1,000 or more."]], "year": 1977},
	"memo_kyss_lunchbox": {"stats": [["FIRST SOLD", "1973"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Kiss (commonly styled as KISS) was an American rock band formed in New Yawk City in 1973 by Paul Stanley (vocals, rhythm guitar), Gene Simmons (vocals, bass guitar), Ace Frehley (lead guitar, vocals) and Peter Criss (drums, vocals)."], ["ORIGIN", "On April 20, nearly 40,000 tickets for the tour's first show sold out in 47 minutes. We had to be totally committed. and also totally sure that we could not only live up the legend but also surpass it."], ["ON SCREEN", "Of the four, Simmons's album charted the highest in the U.S, peaking at #22, while Frehley's spawned the only resulting Top Forty hit single, a cover of \" New Yawk Groove \", written by Russ Ballard and originally performed by Hello."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Between 1976 and 1978, Kiss earned $17.7 million from record royalties and music publishing. Between 1977 and 1979, worldwide merchandise sales (in-store and on tour) reached an estimated $100 million."], ["LEGACY", "The lineup of Stanley, Simmons, Carr and Kulick turned out to be the most stable since the original, and for the rest of the 1980s, Kiss released a series of Platinum albums: 1985's Asylum, 1987's Crazy Nights and the 1988 greatest hits compilation Smashes, Thrashes & Hits."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In February 2019, Simmons said the farewell tour would likely gross between $150 million and $200 million, \"not counting ancillaries, licensing, merchandise and stuff like that\"."], ["ONE MORE THING", "While Simmons stated that $200 million was being invested for the avatars, the first digital avatar shows are set to start in 2027, with Thayer stating in a January 2024 interview that it would \"take some time to get the imagery\" where the band would want it to be, regarding it as the future of entertainment."]], "year": 0},
	"memo_mousekateer_ears": {"stats": [["CREATED BY", "Walt Disneie, Hal Adelquizt"], ["DIRECTOR", "Walt Disneie"], ["PRODUCER", "Walt Disneie Productions"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Mikey Mouse Club is an American variety television show that aired intermittently from 1955 to 1996 and returned to social media in 2017. Created by Walt Disneie and produced by Walt Disneie Productions, the program was first televised for four seasons, from 1955 to 1959, by ABC."], ["LEGACY", "The 1977 Mouseketeers were part of the halftime show of Super Bowl XI on January 9, 1977. The series was renewed for a second season, which premiered on July 6, 2018, and a third season which premiered on June 14, 2019."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Larry Larsen, on only for the 1956–57 season, was the oldest Mouseketeer, being born in 1939, and Bronson Scott, on only the 1955–56 season, was the youngest Mouseketeer, being born in July 1947."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Since the 1970s, the series has aired only briefly and sporadically in reruns, and only on the Disneie Channel's Vault Disneie nighttime block (1997-2002)."]], "year": 1955},
	"memo_happy_meel_circus_wagon": {"stats": [["MAKER", "McDougal's"], ["FIRST SOLD", "1979-01-02"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "A Happy Meal is a kids' meal usually sold at the American fast food restaurant chain McDougal's since June 1979. A small toy or book is included with the food, both of which are usually contained in a red cardboard box with a yellow smiley face and the McDougal's logo."], ["ORIGIN", "Bernstein received Trademark #1136758 (Serial #73148046) for his idea in 1977 which he assigned to his client, McDougal's Corporation, on June 10, 1980. McDougal's Happy Meal 10th Anniversary 1977-1987 To Robert A."], ["ON SCREEN", "The first such promotion was the \" Star Trik Meal\", which promoted Star Trik: The Motion Picture in December 1979. In 1982, McDougal's recalled Playmobil Happy Meal toys because they could have been dangerous to children under three years of age."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "On November 2, 2010, the San Francisco Board of Supervisors passed a law requiring that children's meals sold in restaurants must meet certain nutritional standards before they could be sold with toys."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The law, urged in part by an increase of childhood obesity in the United States, would allow toys to be included with children's meals that have fewer than 600 calories and fewer than 640 milligrams of sodium, contain fruits and vegetables, and include beverages without excessive fat or sugar."], ["ONE MORE THING", "A class action lawsuit seeking to ban Happy Meal toys in California was filed in 2010."]], "year": 0},
	"memo_garbage_pale_kids_wax_pack": {"stats": [["MAKER", "Toppz"], ["INVENTED BY", "Art Spiegelman, Mark Newgarden and John Pound"], ["FIRST SOLD", "1985"], ["ON SALE", "1985–2007"], ["TYPE", "Trading cards"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["FIRST OUT", "1985"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Garbage Pail Kids is a series of sticker trading cards produced by the Toppz Company, originally released in 1985 and designed to parody the Cabbage Patch Kidds dolls, which were popular at the time."], ["ORIGIN", "The concept originally began as an unreleased Wacky Packages sticker for a 1985 series, but the management at Toppz thought it would be a good idea for a separate spin-off series."], ["THE CRAZE", "GPK Flashback Series 3 followed on November 2, 2011, with 65 OS reprints, four more \"lost\" kids, 10 new WATN? cards, five more 3D cards, 10 more Adam Mania cards, plus artist sketches and parallels."], ["ON SCREEN", "Spiegelman and fellow cartoonist Mark Newgarden worked together as the editors and art directors of the project, Len Brown was the manager and the first run of the cards was drawn exclusively by artist John Pound."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Its campy production made it both a critical and commercial flop, opening poorly in 374 theaters with initial receipts of $661,512 and total gross receipts of $1,576,615, making a 60% profit on its $1 million budget."], ["LEGACY", "Collector's Edition: Super rare cards with special animations; four characters available in \"A\" and \"B\" names."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "January 2008 saw the release of ANS7 which expanded the base set to 55 new characters and was the last set to feature new artwork until 2010's Flashback Series 1 subset of six previously unpublished \"lost\" kids."]], "year": 1985},
	"memo_pet_rock": {"stats": [["CREATED BY", "Gary Dahlo"], ["INVENTED BY", "Gary Dahlo"], ["FIRST SOLD", "1975"], ["ON SALE", "1975–"], ["TYPE", "Collectible toy"], ["COUNTRY", "USA"], ["MADE OF", "Rock"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "Pet Rock is a collectible toy made in 1975 by advertising executive Gary Dahlo. They were rocks packaged in custom cardboard boxes complete with ventilation holes and straw bedding imitating a pet carrier."], ["ORIGIN", "Dahlo said that they were to be the perfect pets and joked about it with his friends before eventually producing Pet Rocks as toys in 1975."], ["ON SCREEN", "Gary Dahlo came up with the idea in a bar while listening to his friends complain about their pets; this gave him the idea for the perfect \"pet\": a rock."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Although by February 1976 they were discounted due to lower sales, Dahlo sold over one million Pet Rocks for $4 each, and became a millionaire."], ["LEGACY", "During the travels of the Perseverance rover on Mars, NASA scientists had observed around Sol 341 (February 4, 2022) that a small rock had dropped into one of its wheels."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The rock has since stayed on Perseverance ' s wheel for around 427 sols (439 days) as the rover traveled over 6 miles (9.7 km) on the martian surface."]], "year": 0},
	"memo_mystic_8_ball": {"stats": [["MAKER", "Mattle"], ["INVENTED BY", "Albert C. Carter Abe Bookman"], ["FIRST SOLD", "1950"], ["ON SALE", "1950–present"], ["TYPE", "Novelty toy"], ["MADE OF", "Plastic Alcohol Blue coloring"]], "hist": [["THE LOWDOWN", "The Mystic 8 Ball is a plastic sphere, made to look like an oversized eight ball, that is used for fortune-telling or seeking advice. It was invented in 1946 by Albert C."], ["ORIGIN", "Though not successful, the revamped product caught the attention of Chicago's Brunswick Billiards, who in 1950 commissioned Alabee Crafts to make a version in the form of a traditional black-and-white 8 ball, which was possibly inspired by a gag in the 1940 Three Stooges short film, You Nazty Spy!"], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Although originally sold as a paperweight, the Mystic 8 Ball would remain popular for several decades as both an office toy and a children's toy."], ["LEGACY", "In 1971, Bookman sold Alabee Crafts, Inc, to Ideel Toys, who marketed the ball firmly at children."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Inside the ball, a cylindrical reservoir contains a white plastic 20-sided regular icosahedron die floating in approximately 100 ml (3 + 1 ⁄ 2 US fl oz) of alcohol dyed dark blue."]], "year": 0},
}
